WEBVTT

1
00:00:07,007 --> 00:00:09,009
[night bird calling]

2
00:00:24,649 --> 00:00:26,484
[man] I'm from South Carolina.

3
00:00:26,568 --> 00:00:28,653
Lexington, South Carolina, to be exact.

4
00:00:29,154 --> 00:00:30,363
Born and raised.

5
00:00:30,447 --> 00:00:32,782
[blowing bird call whistle]

6
00:00:32,866 --> 00:00:34,451
My name is Michael Smoak.

7
00:00:34,534 --> 00:00:36,286
Everybody just calls me Smoak.

8
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:40,540
[imitating bird call]

9
00:00:41,124 --> 00:00:43,501
I've been called
by my last name my entire life.

10
00:00:43,585 --> 00:00:46,755
Everybody from when I was a child
has called me Smoak.

11
00:00:47,464 --> 00:00:50,175
If you call me Michael,
I'm in big trouble. So…

12
00:00:53,511 --> 00:00:55,930
[soft, somber music playing]

13
00:00:56,014 --> 00:00:58,975
I was five years old when 9/11 happened.

14
00:01:00,477 --> 00:01:02,270
I didn't really know what was going on,

15
00:01:02,353 --> 00:01:04,397
but I could tell
by the emotion in the room

16
00:01:04,481 --> 00:01:06,775
that something serious had happened.

17
00:01:08,318 --> 00:01:11,613
I was actually home sick
from school that day.

18
00:01:11,696 --> 00:01:13,698
I was at my grandmother's house,

19
00:01:13,782 --> 00:01:18,828
and I remember me, her,
and my granddad watching it on the TV.

20
00:01:18,912 --> 00:01:22,373
I just remember both of them
standing up out of their chairs

21
00:01:22,457 --> 00:01:26,127
and kind of walked towards the TV,
getting closer to it.

22
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:29,964
[Tom Brokaw] <i>It looks like</i>
<i>a big chunk of it has just peeled away.</i>

23
00:01:30,048 --> 00:01:33,259
[reporter] <i>One can only hope that the area</i>
<i>has been evacuated, but of course…</i>

24
00:01:33,343 --> 00:01:36,888
[Smoak] I started to understand 9/11
more and more as I got older.

25
00:01:37,972 --> 00:01:41,142
I understood what happened,
but not really why it happened.

26
00:01:41,643 --> 00:01:42,519
[indistinct dialog]

27
00:01:42,602 --> 00:01:46,272
Later, on YouTube,
I saw videos of the war…

28
00:01:46,356 --> 00:01:47,732
[soldier] Target!

29
00:01:47,816 --> 00:01:49,025
[firing]

30
00:01:50,652 --> 00:01:52,237
[Smoak] …and I wanted to serve my country.

31
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:55,865
I don't even remember
where I heard the term "Marine Corps,"

32
00:01:55,949 --> 00:01:59,119
but I just remember
looking at all the videos

33
00:01:59,202 --> 00:02:01,996
and the uniforms
and all that stuff on the internet.

34
00:02:03,414 --> 00:02:05,166
I was like, "That's gonna be me one day."

35
00:02:06,543 --> 00:02:08,253
"I'm going to be a Marine
when I get older."

36
00:02:08,336 --> 00:02:09,504
[helicopter whirring]

37
00:02:12,966 --> 00:02:16,803
We got told that we were going in
for a non-combative evacuation.

38
00:02:16,886 --> 00:02:19,556
{\an8}[reporter] <i>The war in Afghanistan is over.</i>

39
00:02:19,639 --> 00:02:23,852
{\an8}<i>This puts an end to America's longest war.</i>
<i>Twenty years, 2,400 lives lost.</i>

40
00:02:25,145 --> 00:02:27,647
[Smoak] And then it got pretty crazy.

41
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:31,317
[people clamoring and shouting]

42
00:02:32,026 --> 00:02:34,821
This was nearly 20 years after 9/11.

43
00:02:34,904 --> 00:02:38,491
[clamoring continues]

44
00:02:39,325 --> 00:02:41,870
-[distant siren wailing]
-And what started on 9/11

45
00:02:41,953 --> 00:02:44,247
{\an8}ended right here at Abbey Gate.

46
00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:46,666
[woman screams]

47
00:02:48,042 --> 00:02:52,505
My thoughts were, "What the hell
have we been doing for 20 years?"

48
00:02:53,756 --> 00:02:56,009
If they didn't have the right paperwork,

49
00:02:56,092 --> 00:02:58,720
most of the time,
you're sending them to their death,

50
00:02:58,803 --> 00:03:04,726
which is a pretty crazy responsibility
for a 23-year-old.

51
00:03:04,809 --> 00:03:05,810
[dramatic music playing]

52
00:03:05,894 --> 00:03:07,395
[soldier] Move!

53
00:03:08,855 --> 00:03:09,856
[explosion]

54
00:03:09,939 --> 00:03:13,234
[people clamoring and screaming]

55
00:03:16,237 --> 00:03:22,702
[solemn music playing]

56
00:03:31,169 --> 00:03:33,546
[Garrett M. Graff]
We're at a moment in American life

57
00:03:33,630 --> 00:03:36,090
when the events of 9/11 are beginning

58
00:03:36,174 --> 00:03:41,095
to slip meaningfully
from living memory into history.

59
00:03:41,179 --> 00:03:44,057
[rumbling and whooshing]

60
00:03:45,016 --> 00:03:46,976
At some point in the next couple of years,

61
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:51,606
we will cross the point
where 50% of Americans

62
00:03:51,689 --> 00:03:56,819
were born after 9/11 or were too young
to remember the events of that day.

63
00:03:58,196 --> 00:03:59,948
{\an8}Now they're learning about the attacks

64
00:04:00,031 --> 00:04:02,700
{\an8}and seeing those images from the day
in school.

65
00:04:03,618 --> 00:04:06,287
[student] It's just, like, taught as,

66
00:04:06,371 --> 00:04:09,874
it happened, and we should
just know that it happened.

67
00:04:09,958 --> 00:04:14,045
I haven't really heard of it much
outside of, like, the anniversary of 9/11.

68
00:04:14,128 --> 00:04:17,507
{\an8}It just feels like another part of
the wars that we've learned about

69
00:04:17,590 --> 00:04:19,884
because I wasn't alive for it
or there for it.

70
00:04:23,096 --> 00:04:26,557
[Azmat Khan] On 9/11,
I was in English class.

71
00:04:28,059 --> 00:04:29,727
I was in 11th grade.

72
00:04:30,687 --> 00:04:32,730
I think we were
the only Muslims at school.

73
00:04:34,107 --> 00:04:37,068
[Laurel Homer]
I was ten months old on September 11th.

74
00:04:37,902 --> 00:04:39,946
A lot of times
I feel kind of like collateral.

75
00:04:41,489 --> 00:04:43,408
[Wyatt Wilson]
I had just turned three years old.

76
00:04:43,908 --> 00:04:45,743
I was probably getting my diaper changed.

77
00:04:45,827 --> 00:04:47,537
I was three years old.

78
00:04:48,746 --> 00:04:50,373
[An Nguyen] I just turned four.

79
00:04:51,666 --> 00:04:53,835
That's 25 years past.

80
00:04:53,918 --> 00:04:57,463
It's basically a generation
since the attacks.

81
00:04:58,923 --> 00:05:03,011
It's critical to know
the pain and the grief.

82
00:05:05,388 --> 00:05:09,434
The ramifications of 9/11 are evolving.

83
00:05:10,518 --> 00:05:12,729
And even some consequences from that event

84
00:05:12,812 --> 00:05:15,398
are still unfolding to this day.

85
00:05:15,481 --> 00:05:17,108
[whooshing, then exploding]

86
00:05:19,819 --> 00:05:24,449
[Graff] 25 years later,
the current generation lives in

87
00:05:24,532 --> 00:05:28,036
a world that was created by 9/11.

88
00:05:28,119 --> 00:05:30,121
[somber music playing]

89
00:05:41,674 --> 00:05:45,970
[Anand Gopal] We had a sense back then
that we were at a transformative moment.

90
00:05:46,054 --> 00:05:47,263
And we were.

91
00:05:51,517 --> 00:05:52,518
I was asleep.

92
00:05:53,644 --> 00:05:55,646
I was an undergrad at NYU at that time.

93
00:05:57,440 --> 00:05:59,233
I was living in Lower Manhattan,

94
00:05:59,317 --> 00:06:01,944
and I felt a loud sound.

95
00:06:02,028 --> 00:06:06,574
[airplane approaching]

96
00:06:06,657 --> 00:06:11,954
[airplane gets louder]

97
00:06:13,039 --> 00:06:14,916
[loud explosion]

98
00:06:14,999 --> 00:06:18,503
{\an8}[Stephen McArdle] <i>That was an explosion.</i>
<i>That was an explosion.</i>

99
00:06:18,586 --> 00:06:25,593
{\an8}[indistinct clamoring]

100
00:06:26,719 --> 00:06:28,429
[man] Holy shit!

101
00:06:29,555 --> 00:06:30,765
[Gopal] I turned the TV on.

102
00:06:30,848 --> 00:06:33,101
[reporter] <i>We have a breaking news story</i>
<i>to tell you about.</i>

103
00:06:33,184 --> 00:06:37,855
{\an8}<i>Apparently, a plane has just crashed</i>
<i>into the World Trade Center here in…</i>

104
00:06:37,939 --> 00:06:40,483
{\an8}[Gopal] That's all we had,
is the main television stations.

105
00:06:40,566 --> 00:06:44,237
I don't mean to interrupt the fun,
but this is a breaking news story.

106
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:46,697
A plane has crashed…

107
00:06:47,198 --> 00:06:49,992
-Hold it… Into the World Trade Center.
-[Robin Quivers] You're kidding!

108
00:06:50,076 --> 00:06:51,786
[reporter 2] <i>We have no further details.</i>

109
00:06:51,869 --> 00:06:56,290
<i>We don't know anything about</i>
<i>what they have concluded happened there.</i>

110
00:06:56,374 --> 00:06:59,877
So anyway, we go on now. How are you?

111
00:06:59,961 --> 00:07:02,088
-I know you're in shock.
-I'm really in shock.

112
00:07:02,171 --> 00:07:05,174
{\an8}I've never… I don't know what to say.

113
00:07:05,258 --> 00:07:08,261
{\an8}[reporter 3] <i>CNN Center right now</i>
<i>is just beginning to work on this story,</i>

114
00:07:08,344 --> 00:07:12,223
{\an8}<i>obviously calling our sources and trying</i>
<i>to figure out exactly what happened.</i>

115
00:07:12,306 --> 00:07:14,809
[reporter 4] <i>…fire is</i>
<i>nowhere near under control…</i>

116
00:07:14,892 --> 00:07:17,061
[Jenny Pachucki] For 10 years,
I was an oral historian

117
00:07:17,145 --> 00:07:20,106
and curator at the 9/11 Museum,

118
00:07:20,189 --> 00:07:24,777
recording oral histories with survivors,
first responders, family members.

119
00:07:26,362 --> 00:07:29,157
If you're trying
to have hope for a generation,

120
00:07:29,866 --> 00:07:32,660
I think that helping them
understand what happened

121
00:07:32,743 --> 00:07:36,122
is an important part
of creating good for the future.

122
00:07:37,498 --> 00:07:41,252
When these people become real
and this event becomes personal,

123
00:07:42,712 --> 00:07:45,673
that's the most powerful way
to understand, ultimately,

124
00:07:45,756 --> 00:07:47,967
what this was and why it mattered.

125
00:07:49,010 --> 00:07:53,097
[sirens wailing]

126
00:07:54,724 --> 00:07:57,268
[police operator] <i>Police operator 133,</i>
<i>what's the emergency?</i>

127
00:07:57,351 --> 00:08:00,938
[Battaglia] <i>I'm on the 100th floor of</i>
<i>the World Trade Center with 30 people.</i>

128
00:08:01,022 --> 00:08:02,940
[Pachucki] People are calling 911.

129
00:08:03,691 --> 00:08:05,651
They're reporting being trapped.

130
00:08:05,735 --> 00:08:07,153
They're reporting being stuck.

131
00:08:07,236 --> 00:08:09,363
[operator] <i>How are you doing?</i>
<i>This is the fire department.</i>

132
00:08:09,447 --> 00:08:11,574
[Battaglia] <i>Hi. It's hard to talk.</i>

133
00:08:11,657 --> 00:08:13,117
-[operator] <i>Yeah.</i>
<i>-A lot of smoke.</i>

134
00:08:13,201 --> 00:08:14,410
-[operator] <i>Right.</i>
<i>-The heat…</i>

135
00:08:14,494 --> 00:08:17,121
<i>We're all overreacting.</i>
<i>Please come right now.</i>

136
00:08:17,205 --> 00:08:19,999
-[operator] <i>What floor are you on?</i>
-[Battaglia] <i>100 Northeast.</i>

137
00:08:20,082 --> 00:08:21,125
[operator] <i>All right.</i>

138
00:08:21,209 --> 00:08:23,085
[people clamoring and coughing]

139
00:08:23,169 --> 00:08:25,254
[woman] <i>It's too hot!</i>

140
00:08:25,338 --> 00:08:27,048
[operator] <i>What's your phone number there?</i>

141
00:08:27,131 --> 00:08:28,633
[Battaglia] <i>I'm on a cell phone.</i>

142
00:08:28,716 --> 00:08:31,177
[operator] <i>All right, no problem.</i>
<i>We'll get to you.</i>

143
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:34,096
-[Battaglia] <i>Please, immediately.</i>
-[operator] <i>They're on the way.</i>

144
00:08:34,180 --> 00:08:36,724
[Pachucki] Really, the only thing
that people can say is,

145
00:08:36,807 --> 00:08:39,810
"Hang on, we're coming for you.
We'll be there as soon as we can."

146
00:08:44,732 --> 00:08:48,861
Our son, Brad, had just graduated
from Bucknell University.

147
00:08:50,029 --> 00:08:55,618
On September 11th, he was working
on the 89th floor of the South Tower.

148
00:08:56,953 --> 00:08:58,663
{\an8}<i>Hey, Mom, it's Brad.</i>

149
00:08:58,746 --> 00:09:01,582
{\an8}<i>I just wanted to call</i>
<i>and let you know, I'm sure that you heard</i>

150
00:09:01,666 --> 00:09:04,460
{\an8}<i>that a plane crashed</i>
<i>into World Trade Center One.</i>

151
00:09:04,544 --> 00:09:06,921
<i>We're fine,</i>
<i>we're in World Trade Center Two.</i>

152
00:09:07,004 --> 00:09:10,466
<i>I'm not…</i>
<i>I'm obviously alive and well over here.</i>

153
00:09:10,550 --> 00:09:13,261
<i>But obviously a pretty scary experience.</i>

154
00:09:15,429 --> 00:09:20,142
[Pachucki] For most people, it was just
a confusing, atrocious accident.

155
00:09:23,437 --> 00:09:25,439
[woman] They believe it was an airplane.

156
00:09:25,523 --> 00:09:27,567
I'm going to go watch the news now.

157
00:09:28,276 --> 00:09:32,029
We thought we were invincible,
but we also thought we were invulnerable.

158
00:09:32,113 --> 00:09:34,365
In other words,
that no one could attack us.

159
00:09:36,075 --> 00:09:39,245
9/11 shatters that complacency.

160
00:09:39,328 --> 00:09:41,706
[news playing in background]

161
00:09:41,789 --> 00:09:44,083
[woman on phone] <i>…get through,</i>
<i>and I'm far away right now,</i>

162
00:09:44,166 --> 00:09:46,627
<i>I thought it might be</i>
<i>a little dangerous to get too close.</i>

163
00:09:46,711 --> 00:09:48,129
<i>I saw lots of debris coming…</i>

164
00:09:48,212 --> 00:09:53,384
[Graff] You see in that moment
between 8:46 and 9:03

165
00:09:53,884 --> 00:09:56,846
how innocent America actually is.

166
00:10:04,562 --> 00:10:09,567
In those 17 minutes,
America still felt innocent.

167
00:10:10,651 --> 00:10:14,238
{\an8}[reporter] <i>And as fire crews</i>
<i>are descending on this area,</i>

168
00:10:15,489 --> 00:10:19,452
{\an8}<i>it does not appear that there's</i>
<i>any kind of an effort up there yet.</i>

169
00:10:19,535 --> 00:10:21,078
{\an8}<i>Now remember-- Oh my God!</i>

170
00:10:21,746 --> 00:10:23,456
{\an8}[newsreader] <i>My God. My…</i>

171
00:10:23,539 --> 00:10:25,416
{\an8}[newsreader 2]
<i>That looks like a second plane.</i>

172
00:10:25,958 --> 00:10:27,543
[plane approaching]

173
00:10:35,843 --> 00:10:38,846
[woman on phone] <i>It was a jet. It was</i>
<i>a very large plane. It was going fast.</i>

174
00:10:38,929 --> 00:10:42,224
<i>It went past the Ritz-Carlton Hotel</i>
<i>that's being built in Battery Park.</i>

175
00:10:42,308 --> 00:10:44,060
<i>It went through right past, almost hit it…</i>

176
00:10:44,143 --> 00:10:45,811
-[people screaming]
<i>-…and then went in.</i>

177
00:10:46,771 --> 00:10:50,399
[newsreader 2] <i>So this looks like</i>
<i>it is some sort of a concerted effort</i>

178
00:10:50,483 --> 00:10:55,029
<i>to attack the World Trade Center</i>
<i>that is underway in downtown New York.</i>

179
00:10:55,112 --> 00:10:57,865
[sirens wailing]

180
00:10:57,948 --> 00:10:59,617
[man] My name is Andrew Ansbro.

181
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:02,244
I joined the FDNY in February of 2001.

182
00:11:02,995 --> 00:11:04,914
I was assigned to Engine 58 in Harlem.

183
00:11:06,749 --> 00:11:08,876
On September 11th, I was 29 years old.

184
00:11:09,877 --> 00:11:11,420
I was the new guy.

185
00:11:11,504 --> 00:11:14,173
You know, I was actually
getting off a 24-hour shift

186
00:11:14,256 --> 00:11:16,342
when, uh, you know, the first plane hit.

187
00:11:16,425 --> 00:11:19,845
We got assigned to the second
fifth alarm, which was the South Tower.

188
00:11:19,929 --> 00:11:23,182
-[fire alarm bell ringing]
-You get the alarm, get the adrenaline.

189
00:11:23,724 --> 00:11:26,310
I thought, "Here we are,
we're going down there to put it out."

190
00:11:26,394 --> 00:11:27,395
[siren wailing]

191
00:11:27,478 --> 00:11:29,230
The street was very different.

192
00:11:29,313 --> 00:11:33,359
Everyone got out of the way of the rig,
and, uh, people were cheering for us.

193
00:11:33,442 --> 00:11:36,737
It was like being a gladiator
heading down to battle.

194
00:11:36,821 --> 00:11:40,032
Everyone was, you know,
cheering and clapping as we're going.

195
00:11:40,116 --> 00:11:42,535
And I just remember
passing a block in the 70s

196
00:11:42,618 --> 00:11:47,748
and focusing on this one person who was
standing there looking at us, crying.

197
00:11:47,832 --> 00:11:48,916
And then it hit me.

198
00:11:49,625 --> 00:11:52,670
[indistinct dialog over dispatch radio]

199
00:11:52,753 --> 00:11:53,754
Shit.

200
00:11:56,090 --> 00:11:58,050
[Ansbro] And then the lieutenant said,

201
00:11:58,134 --> 00:11:59,719
"Listen, make no mistake,

202
00:11:59,802 --> 00:12:02,096
everyone above that fire
is dead or gonna die."

203
00:12:02,179 --> 00:12:03,931
[sirens wailing]

204
00:12:04,014 --> 00:12:06,058
[newsreader] <i>Thousands,</i>
<i>tens of thousands of people</i>

205
00:12:06,142 --> 00:12:08,352
<i>actually work inside those two buildings</i>

206
00:12:08,436 --> 00:12:10,688
<i>in the heart</i>
<i>of New York's financial district.</i>

207
00:12:15,401 --> 00:12:18,237
{\an8}<i>You could see the fire,</i>
<i>the flames, and all the smoke.</i>

208
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:20,656
{\an8}<i>And I said, "Oh my God, my wife is there."</i>

209
00:12:21,282 --> 00:12:23,576
{\an8}<i>She worked on the 96th floor,</i>

210
00:12:24,118 --> 00:12:26,245
{\an8}<i>but I was hoping for the best.</i>

211
00:12:29,707 --> 00:12:32,543
<i>My daughter was two years old.</i>

212
00:12:33,878 --> 00:12:37,673
<i>A few days later, she just started</i>
<i>screaming and crying, calling her mother.</i>

213
00:12:37,757 --> 00:12:39,759
<i>That's when it hit me.</i>

214
00:12:39,842 --> 00:12:44,180
[sirens wailing]

215
00:12:44,263 --> 00:12:46,140
[Pachucki] People are
coming down the stairwells.

216
00:12:48,768 --> 00:12:52,438
It is smoky, depending on where you were.

217
00:12:53,147 --> 00:12:55,191
It is crowded.

218
00:12:56,317 --> 00:12:59,779
There's women wearing
full stiletto heels walking down

219
00:13:01,030 --> 00:13:02,907
many, many stairs.

220
00:13:03,616 --> 00:13:07,328
People describe people helping each other
in the stairwells.

221
00:13:11,665 --> 00:13:16,629
There were stations set up by responders
to provide people with oxygen.

222
00:13:16,712 --> 00:13:18,714
People were being handed water.

223
00:13:20,299 --> 00:13:22,676
There's this moment
when the first responders

224
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:25,679
are gathering
at the incident command post,

225
00:13:25,763 --> 00:13:29,141
and they're preparing to go up
and developing their strategy.

226
00:13:29,809 --> 00:13:33,521
And somebody looked at the little group
that was about to go up the stairs

227
00:13:33,604 --> 00:13:36,524
and said, "We may not live through today."

228
00:13:36,607 --> 00:13:38,275
[sniffles, stifles sob]

229
00:13:39,401 --> 00:13:42,613
[crying] And they took a moment
to shake each other's hands.

230
00:13:52,665 --> 00:13:54,333
[sirens wailing]

231
00:13:54,416 --> 00:13:56,877
[Rudy Giuliani] All that we know…
All that we know right now

232
00:13:56,961 --> 00:14:00,923
{\an8}is that two airplanes struck the two
large towers of the World Trade Center.

233
00:14:01,006 --> 00:14:03,676
[man on radio]
Stand by. Just stand by. We'll get 'em.

234
00:14:03,759 --> 00:14:06,679
[Pachucki] There was no question
that the city was being attacked.

235
00:14:07,555 --> 00:14:09,890
Later, we realized it was the country.

236
00:14:17,439 --> 00:14:20,609
Another plane is headed
towards Washington, D.C.

237
00:14:23,904 --> 00:14:25,114
[man] I'm An Nguyen.

238
00:14:25,614 --> 00:14:27,783
I am the son of Khang Nguyen,

239
00:14:27,867 --> 00:14:31,996
who was working
at the Pentagon on September 11th.

240
00:14:33,122 --> 00:14:37,293
My father, he was born in South Vietnam.

241
00:14:38,002 --> 00:14:42,590
And then he came to America
after the Fall of Saigon in 1975.

242
00:14:44,216 --> 00:14:47,636
But he is a testament of
basically the American dream.

243
00:14:48,762 --> 00:14:50,931
There were opportunities here in the US

244
00:14:51,015 --> 00:14:54,685
that was not given to him when he was
younger back in Vietnam.

245
00:14:55,436 --> 00:14:57,730
My father was an electrical engineer

246
00:14:57,813 --> 00:15:00,316
with the Navy Command Center
at the Pentagon.

247
00:15:01,317 --> 00:15:03,068
I'd just turned four.

248
00:15:03,694 --> 00:15:05,863
I was in preschool,

249
00:15:05,946 --> 00:15:07,489
and on that Tuesday morning,

250
00:15:07,573 --> 00:15:10,659
when my mom was taking me out
to the bus to go to preschool,

251
00:15:10,743 --> 00:15:12,494
my father ran towards the bus

252
00:15:12,578 --> 00:15:16,582
to just say goodbye to me one more,
just one last… one last time.

253
00:15:18,500 --> 00:15:22,671
[melancholy music playing]

254
00:15:25,257 --> 00:15:28,093
{\an8}[plane approaching]

255
00:15:33,849 --> 00:15:39,396
[Pachucki] At 9:37, Flight 77 crashed
into the E ring of the Pentagon.

256
00:15:40,648 --> 00:15:42,816
[dispatcher] <i>Engine 101, </i>[indistinct]<i>…</i>

257
00:15:44,276 --> 00:15:48,155
[firefighter] <i>105 is on the scene.</i>
<i>I have a </i>[indistinct]…

258
00:15:49,365 --> 00:15:51,283
<i>…transport, the Pentagon.</i>

259
00:15:52,284 --> 00:15:54,912
[siren wailing]

260
00:15:54,995 --> 00:15:58,958
[newsreader] <i>It would appear that</i>
<i>there has been another major explosion,</i>

261
00:15:59,041 --> 00:16:00,834
<i>this one in the nation's capital.</i>

262
00:16:00,918 --> 00:16:05,506
<i>You are looking at a scene</i>
<i>of apparent blast aftermath.</i>

263
00:16:05,589 --> 00:16:08,175
<i>There is smoke in the air</i>
<i>over the Pentagon.</i>

264
00:16:11,178 --> 00:16:13,389
[Nguyen] I just went home
when preschool ended.

265
00:16:14,473 --> 00:16:19,353
And I was just waiting for my father
to show up on the driveway with his car.

266
00:16:19,436 --> 00:16:21,105
And I just kept waiting and waiting…

267
00:16:24,066 --> 00:16:26,068
just hoping that he would come home.

268
00:16:29,697 --> 00:16:32,825
With the days that followed,
especially the funeral,

269
00:16:32,908 --> 00:16:36,161
that's when I realized
that he's not coming back home.

270
00:16:38,247 --> 00:16:41,583
An outpour of tears started to show.

271
00:16:43,877 --> 00:16:47,131
Terrorism is a word
that you will never understand

272
00:16:47,214 --> 00:16:50,718
in any visceral way
until you experience it.

273
00:16:52,261 --> 00:16:55,222
I did not know how to come to terms

274
00:16:55,305 --> 00:16:57,474
with a loss of that magnitude,

275
00:16:58,726 --> 00:17:01,270
but I knew that
my life would change forever.

276
00:17:05,441 --> 00:17:09,153
And now I'm trying to make an identity
within a world

277
00:17:09,236 --> 00:17:11,530
without having my father by my side.

278
00:17:13,657 --> 00:17:19,288
[sirens wailing]

279
00:17:19,371 --> 00:17:21,874
[Pachucki] The Pentagon is a private area.

280
00:17:21,957 --> 00:17:23,417
It's a lower building.

281
00:17:24,501 --> 00:17:26,587
And the force of the plane,

282
00:17:26,670 --> 00:17:28,464
the amount of jet fuel,

283
00:17:29,214 --> 00:17:32,217
and the size and structure of the building

284
00:17:32,718 --> 00:17:34,970
creates like an inferno.

285
00:17:35,054 --> 00:17:38,724
[fire roaring and crackling]

286
00:17:41,852 --> 00:17:43,353
{\an8}And at the same time,

287
00:17:43,437 --> 00:17:46,398
{\an8}the South Tower was burning
for 56 minutes.

288
00:17:49,818 --> 00:17:52,362
The impact zone was a little bit lower.

289
00:17:57,159 --> 00:18:02,956
So an hour burning
at extremely hot temperatures,

290
00:18:03,040 --> 00:18:06,960
ultimately the building
began to pull into itself.

291
00:18:07,961 --> 00:18:11,256
[tragic music playing]

292
00:18:13,467 --> 00:18:17,387
At some point there was
a deep rumble in the earth.

293
00:18:17,471 --> 00:18:18,764
It felt like an earthquake.

294
00:18:18,847 --> 00:18:20,849
-[people clamoring]
-[sirens wailing]

295
00:18:20,933 --> 00:18:23,227
[car horns honking]

296
00:18:25,896 --> 00:18:28,816
[Ansbro] We saw a whole bunch of cops
lined up like a roll call.

297
00:18:28,899 --> 00:18:30,442
Then they just started running.

298
00:18:30,943 --> 00:18:33,112
We thought something
was falling out of the sky.

299
00:18:33,195 --> 00:18:36,365
Or there was a disturbance
because they all ran north.

300
00:18:37,241 --> 00:18:40,285
What we didn't know was that
the police officers in the helicopter

301
00:18:40,369 --> 00:18:41,829
had radioed down to them.

302
00:18:41,912 --> 00:18:44,414
The top of the South Tower
had started to twist.

303
00:18:44,498 --> 00:18:45,791
And they could see it.

304
00:18:49,044 --> 00:18:50,462
They told them to evacuate.

305
00:18:52,172 --> 00:18:53,757
None of us got that message.

306
00:18:56,760 --> 00:18:58,595
{\an8}[Pachucki] There was a man
named Sean Rooney

307
00:18:58,679 --> 00:19:01,598
{\an8}who called his wife, Beverly Eckert.

308
00:19:03,725 --> 00:19:07,104
{\an8}They connect over the phone,
and he's trapped.

309
00:19:07,980 --> 00:19:10,065
[Beverly Eckert]
<i>It was about 9:30 a.m. when he called.</i>

310
00:19:11,024 --> 00:19:13,819
{\an8}<i>And he told me he was on the 105th floor.</i>

311
00:19:16,989 --> 00:19:19,116
{\an8}<i>And he'd been trying to find a way out.</i>

312
00:19:20,367 --> 00:19:25,372
{\an8}<i>He told me that he hadn't had any success</i>
<i>and now the stairwell was full of smoke.</i>

313
00:19:28,083 --> 00:19:30,377
<i>We stopped talking about escape routes.</i>

314
00:19:32,796 --> 00:19:34,464
<i>And then we just began talking about</i>

315
00:19:34,548 --> 00:19:37,843
<i>all the happiness we shared</i>
<i>during our lives together.</i>

316
00:19:40,846 --> 00:19:43,849
<i>He just kept whispering "I love you"</i>
<i>over and over.</i>

317
00:19:45,309 --> 00:19:47,394
<i>Then I heard a sharp crack,</i>

318
00:19:47,477 --> 00:19:50,314
<i>followed by the sound of an avalanche.</i>

319
00:19:50,981 --> 00:19:51,982
Take two.

320
00:19:52,065 --> 00:19:53,775
Take two and two, one.

321
00:19:53,859 --> 00:19:56,570
This is as close as we can get
to the base of the World Trade Center.

322
00:19:56,653 --> 00:19:58,405
-You see the firemen assembled here…
-[bang]

323
00:19:58,488 --> 00:20:00,407
-…the police officers, FBI agents.
-[another bang]

324
00:20:00,490 --> 00:20:02,993
-And you can see the two towers.
-[louder bang]

325
00:20:03,076 --> 00:20:06,205
A huge explosion now,
raining debris on all of us.

326
00:20:06,288 --> 00:20:07,915
We better get out of the way!

327
00:20:10,125 --> 00:20:12,753
Come on, Glenn.
Come on, Glenn, you can do it, man.

328
00:20:14,254 --> 00:20:16,215
[Ansbro] There was
this loud crack and a boom.

329
00:20:16,298 --> 00:20:18,884
And you know…
Bop-bop-bop-bop-bop-bop-bop-bop.

330
00:20:18,967 --> 00:20:21,303
And that was the floors pancaking
and coming down.

331
00:20:21,386 --> 00:20:24,014
[people screaming]

332
00:20:24,097 --> 00:20:27,476
The street got dark
and went from day to night in seconds.

333
00:20:28,101 --> 00:20:32,773
So, instinctively, myself and whoever
came from the south, we ran south.

334
00:20:33,815 --> 00:20:34,942
[whooshing]

335
00:20:35,025 --> 00:20:37,569
[reporter in Japanese]
Just now with a loud crash

336
00:20:37,653 --> 00:20:41,198
a building has collapsed!

337
00:20:41,907 --> 00:20:44,701
[Ansbro] When the building hit the ground,
it kind of came over us

338
00:20:44,785 --> 00:20:46,995
and the air pressure
from the compressed air

339
00:20:47,079 --> 00:20:48,372
-blew the windows in.
-[whooshing]

340
00:20:48,455 --> 00:20:50,415
[man] Oh shit. Oh shit.

341
00:20:50,499 --> 00:20:51,708
[Ansbro] We were airborne.

342
00:20:51,792 --> 00:20:54,586
I don't know how far I flew,
but I landed in a pile of people.

343
00:20:54,670 --> 00:21:00,550
And, uh, immediately your next breath
was like sucking in a cup full of sand.

344
00:21:00,634 --> 00:21:01,551
It was just… [choking]

345
00:21:02,344 --> 00:21:04,972
And you're getting hit with,
you know, little pieces of debris.

346
00:21:05,055 --> 00:21:07,599
And you just say to yourself,
"This is how it ends."

347
00:21:07,683 --> 00:21:08,892
[man] I hope I live.

348
00:21:08,976 --> 00:21:11,186
I hope I live. It's coming down on me.

349
00:21:11,812 --> 00:21:16,233
[Gopal] I ran just ahead of the front
of the smoke and I got under a car.

350
00:21:16,316 --> 00:21:18,860
There was a subway grate under the car,

351
00:21:18,944 --> 00:21:23,573
and I used the subway grate to breathe
for the next three or four hours.

352
00:21:23,657 --> 00:21:26,285
It was too thick to see outside.

353
00:21:26,368 --> 00:21:28,036
[people clamoring]

354
00:21:28,662 --> 00:21:31,290
[Pachucki] At 10:28 a.m.,

355
00:21:31,373 --> 00:21:33,000
the North Tower collapsed,

356
00:21:33,500 --> 00:21:35,752
killing about 1,500 people

357
00:21:35,836 --> 00:21:38,505
who were unable
to make it out of the building.

358
00:21:39,214 --> 00:21:43,719
-[loud bang and rumbling]
-[people screaming and exclaiming]

359
00:21:46,972 --> 00:21:51,310
[sirens wailing]

360
00:21:52,144 --> 00:21:54,688
[woman] Miss, get in here!
Get away from the glass.

361
00:21:55,314 --> 00:21:56,815
[woman 2] All my shit is out there!

362
00:21:56,898 --> 00:21:58,191
My shit is out there!

363
00:21:58,275 --> 00:21:59,860
[woman] You want your life or your stuff?

364
00:21:59,943 --> 00:22:01,653
[man] Wanna die?
Don't worry about your shit.

365
00:22:01,737 --> 00:22:04,031
[woman] The building,
watch the debris come down the street.

366
00:22:04,114 --> 00:22:07,242
-Don't open the door.
-[man] Get away from the windows.

367
00:22:07,784 --> 00:22:10,746
-[woman] Don't open the door!
-[man] Here it comes! Get behind!

368
00:22:10,829 --> 00:22:12,956
[woman 2] Oh my God. Oh my God!

369
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:15,959
-[man] Look at the shadow of death.
-[woman 2 screams] Oh my God!

370
00:22:16,043 --> 00:22:17,669
[rumbling and whooshing]

371
00:22:17,753 --> 00:22:20,088
You're right!
Oh, my God, you saved my life!

372
00:22:20,172 --> 00:22:22,466
-[man] I told you--
-[woman 2] You saved my life, thank you!

373
00:22:22,549 --> 00:22:27,179
-[sirens blaring]
-[fire truck honking]

374
00:22:27,262 --> 00:22:30,432
[reporter] <i>Words cannot describe it,</i>
<i>except to say that the towers are gone.</i>

375
00:22:30,515 --> 00:22:34,603
<i>Church Street is littered with people</i>
<i>still lingering, uh, looking, watching.</i>

376
00:22:34,686 --> 00:22:36,271
<i>They don't know what to do.</i>

377
00:22:36,355 --> 00:22:39,066
<i>Some people are down</i>
<i>on their knees praying.</i>

378
00:22:39,149 --> 00:22:42,027
[anchor] <i>You're looking at the collapse</i>
<i>of one of those towers right now.</i>

379
00:22:42,110 --> 00:22:45,447
<i>We now have an AP news alert</i>
<i>out of Pittsburgh.</i>

380
00:22:45,530 --> 00:22:48,450
<i>Officials at Somerset County Airport</i>
<i>are confirming</i>

381
00:22:48,533 --> 00:22:51,370
<i>the crash of a large plane</i>
<i>just north of the airport.</i>

382
00:22:51,453 --> 00:22:56,833
[somber music playing]

383
00:23:01,922 --> 00:23:03,256
I'm Laurel Homer.

384
00:23:03,340 --> 00:23:06,009
I was 10 months old on September 11th.

385
00:23:07,302 --> 00:23:09,304
[woman] Is that on his wedding day?

386
00:23:09,388 --> 00:23:10,305
[Homer] Yeah.

387
00:23:11,473 --> 00:23:14,434
I'm the daughter of Leroy Homer.

388
00:23:21,358 --> 00:23:24,778
My dad was the copilot on Flight 93.

389
00:23:28,949 --> 00:23:32,244
[Pachucki] There were
44 people on Flight 93.

390
00:23:34,621 --> 00:23:38,625
And because the flight
is already underway,

391
00:23:38,708 --> 00:23:41,211
a lot of the passengers on the flight

392
00:23:41,294 --> 00:23:44,464
are getting information from the outside

393
00:23:44,548 --> 00:23:47,092
about the attacks unfolding in New York,

394
00:23:47,175 --> 00:23:48,760
and then later the Pentagon.

395
00:23:50,470 --> 00:23:55,016
There were 37 calls
that were exchanged on that flight,

396
00:23:55,100 --> 00:23:56,893
messages that were left.

397
00:23:58,437 --> 00:24:00,021
[Linda Gronlund] <i>Elsa, it's Lin.</i>

398
00:24:00,105 --> 00:24:04,693
<i>Um, I only have a minute.</i>
<i>I'm on United 93 and it's been hijacked</i>

399
00:24:04,776 --> 00:24:07,446
<i>by terrorists who say they have a bomb.</i>

400
00:24:07,529 --> 00:24:11,199
<i>Apparently they've flown a couple of</i>
<i>planes into the World Trade Center,</i>

401
00:24:11,283 --> 00:24:13,952
<i>and it looks like they're going</i>
<i>to take this one down as well.</i>

402
00:24:14,035 --> 00:24:16,621
<i>Mostly I just wanted to say</i>
<i>I love you… </i>[crying]

403
00:24:16,705 --> 00:24:18,832
<i>…and I'm going to miss you.</i>

404
00:24:20,041 --> 00:24:21,710
<i>And, and…</i>

405
00:24:21,793 --> 00:24:24,713
<i>please give my love to Mom and Dad, and…</i>

406
00:24:25,755 --> 00:24:27,174
[shuddering breath]

407
00:24:28,550 --> 00:24:31,344
<i>Mostly I just love you,</i>
<i>and I just wanted to tell you that.</i>

408
00:24:31,428 --> 00:24:35,599
<i>I don't know if I'm going to get</i>
<i>a chance to tell you that again or not.</i>

409
00:24:36,725 --> 00:24:40,729
[Pachucki] The assumed target
we now know was the United States Capitol.

410
00:24:44,733 --> 00:24:48,653
They understood that
they were on a suicide mission

411
00:24:48,737 --> 00:24:53,950
and they understood pretty quickly
that it was up to them to do something.

412
00:24:54,784 --> 00:25:01,625
[man] The, uh, remains of an airplane
crash over on, uh, Lambertsville Road.

413
00:25:04,169 --> 00:25:07,923
[Pachucki] And the passengers decide
with the crew to charge the cockpit.

414
00:25:09,674 --> 00:25:14,763
Ultimately, the hijackers made
the decision to down the plane.

415
00:25:19,643 --> 00:25:24,648
[Homer] They diverted the plane from where
the terrorists wanted it to crash into,

416
00:25:24,731 --> 00:25:28,485
and they saved a lot of people
in doing that.

417
00:25:33,073 --> 00:25:37,744
It's hard. Like, obviously I'm grateful
that more devastation wasn't caused,

418
00:25:37,827 --> 00:25:39,454
but at the same time, like,

419
00:25:39,538 --> 00:25:41,456
I still lost him, so…

420
00:25:42,541 --> 00:25:45,377
it's kind of a… struggle,

421
00:25:45,460 --> 00:25:48,588
because it's like, I know he was a hero,
but I wish he didn't have to be.

422
00:25:50,340 --> 00:25:52,759
My mom, she told me at one point

423
00:25:52,842 --> 00:25:55,387
she thought
she was going to die of heartbreak.

424
00:25:57,264 --> 00:25:59,516
She was, like, devastated…

425
00:26:01,476 --> 00:26:05,397
and she has this young kid
that she needs to take care of.

426
00:26:06,273 --> 00:26:10,193
My entire life was
kind of built around this.

427
00:26:11,570 --> 00:26:13,905
I'm still struggling so much,

428
00:26:13,989 --> 00:26:17,033
[voice breaking]
and it has taken over my whole…

429
00:26:17,951 --> 00:26:18,868
my whole life.

430
00:26:18,952 --> 00:26:24,624
[siren wailing in distance]

431
00:26:25,792 --> 00:26:27,002
[George W. Bush] Good evening.

432
00:26:27,627 --> 00:26:30,088
Today our fellow citizens,

433
00:26:30,171 --> 00:26:31,506
our way of life,

434
00:26:31,590 --> 00:26:34,426
our very freedom came under attack

435
00:26:34,509 --> 00:26:38,096
in a series of deliberate
and deadly terrorist acts.

436
00:26:38,179 --> 00:26:43,143
-[indistinct dialog]
-[sirens wailing]

437
00:26:44,227 --> 00:26:48,773
[Graff] There are four attacks,
and almost 3,000 people die.

438
00:26:53,194 --> 00:26:58,491
But what that misses is what it was like
to live and experience 9/11.

439
00:26:58,575 --> 00:27:00,785
[woman sobbing]

440
00:27:03,997 --> 00:27:09,753
That we miss the fear, the chaos,
the confusion and the trauma of that day.

441
00:27:09,836 --> 00:27:11,421
[person sobbing]

442
00:27:11,504 --> 00:27:13,298
[man] They say someone needs help.

443
00:27:14,341 --> 00:27:16,843
[Graff] We didn't know
when the attacks began.

444
00:27:16,926 --> 00:27:18,094
[woman] …needs some water.

445
00:27:18,178 --> 00:27:20,847
[Graff] We didn't know
when the attacks were over.

446
00:27:20,930 --> 00:27:23,058
We didn't know for much of the day

447
00:27:23,141 --> 00:27:25,435
how many attacks there had been.

448
00:27:28,188 --> 00:27:32,692
We also didn't know whether
there was another wave of attacks coming

449
00:27:32,776 --> 00:27:34,527
in the afternoon of September 11th,

450
00:27:34,611 --> 00:27:36,363
didn't know what was coming
on September 12th.

451
00:27:36,446 --> 00:27:40,742
We didn't know
what was coming in October or in 2002.

452
00:27:45,830 --> 00:27:50,585
If you miss the fear, the chaos,
the confusion and the trauma,

453
00:27:50,669 --> 00:27:57,425
you miss the entire reason that the US
ends up responding in the way that we did.

454
00:27:58,677 --> 00:28:01,805
[protesters shouting]

455
00:28:06,226 --> 00:28:11,064
{\an8}This was an epochal change
in American life.

456
00:28:13,692 --> 00:28:16,695
The feeling of violation

457
00:28:17,529 --> 00:28:20,865
made it feel like anything could happen.

458
00:28:22,534 --> 00:28:24,619
Like the pit had opened up.

459
00:28:25,453 --> 00:28:29,332
The skies had rained fire,
and who knows what could come next.

460
00:28:31,710 --> 00:28:34,337
[Christian Sanchez]
It felt justified to go.

461
00:28:34,421 --> 00:28:37,716
Like, if we got attacked
and they killed a bunch of our people,

462
00:28:37,799 --> 00:28:40,885
{\an8}obviously we should bring some justice
to those that did it.

463
00:28:44,597 --> 00:28:46,433
[man] It's like a war
right now in the world.

464
00:28:46,516 --> 00:28:48,101
-[man 2] This is gonna start a war.
-Yeah.

465
00:28:48,184 --> 00:28:49,769
-[man 2] This is gonna start a war.
-Yeah.

466
00:28:49,853 --> 00:28:51,896
It's gonna start a war. Sure, man. Hey.

467
00:28:53,022 --> 00:28:55,316
[Smoak] I heard grownups talk about it,

468
00:28:55,400 --> 00:29:00,280
so I started to understand more and more
as I got older that what happened,

469
00:29:00,363 --> 00:29:01,614
not really why it happened,

470
00:29:01,698 --> 00:29:04,242
but other than they didn't like Americans.

471
00:29:07,746 --> 00:29:10,707
[Homer] The way that
my mom described what happened

472
00:29:10,790 --> 00:29:14,002
was that there were bad men on his plane.

473
00:29:17,338 --> 00:29:20,008
{\an8}[Nguyen] I knew that
the terrorists were involved.

474
00:29:20,592 --> 00:29:22,302
I know they were coming
from the Middle East,

475
00:29:22,385 --> 00:29:26,347
but I didn't really fully understand,
what was the intention?

476
00:29:27,932 --> 00:29:29,601
Like, what was their goals?

477
00:29:34,564 --> 00:29:38,234
[Bush] Tonight I ask for your prayers
for all those who grieve…

478
00:29:40,361 --> 00:29:42,989
for the children whose worlds
have been shattered,

479
00:29:43,740 --> 00:29:47,410
for all whose sense of safety and security
has been threatened…

480
00:29:49,537 --> 00:29:53,291
and I pray they will be comforted
by a power greater than any of us.

481
00:29:53,374 --> 00:29:56,002
Spoken through the ages in Psalm 23,

482
00:29:56,753 --> 00:30:00,173
"Even though I walk through
the valley of the shadow of death,

483
00:30:00,256 --> 00:30:01,674
I fear no evil,

484
00:30:02,175 --> 00:30:03,593
for you are with me."

485
00:30:13,770 --> 00:30:17,398
[Dr. Joseph Geraci] It just kind of broke
my heart, seeing the posters and the signs

486
00:30:17,482 --> 00:30:20,068
for people looking
for their family members.

487
00:30:21,277 --> 00:30:23,071
The towers were still smoldering,

488
00:30:23,154 --> 00:30:25,573
the rubble was
still smoldering at that time.

489
00:30:26,533 --> 00:30:29,702
{\an8}You know, I don't think
that I had ever felt such a calling.

490
00:30:29,786 --> 00:30:31,454
{\an8}Like what my life was for

491
00:30:31,538 --> 00:30:34,374
{\an8}is like, you know,
we needed to help be part of the solution

492
00:30:34,457 --> 00:30:38,002
and we needed to help to find
the individuals who had done this.

493
00:30:45,051 --> 00:30:48,638
[Jack Goldsmith] Within days,
I think three days after 9/11,

494
00:30:48,721 --> 00:30:53,268
{\an8}there was just a lot of panic
and uncertainty and ignorance and fear.

495
00:30:54,644 --> 00:30:58,398
Immediately, the people in the White House
and in the Justice Department realized

496
00:30:58,481 --> 00:31:02,193
that they needed
robust authorities to respond to it.

497
00:31:02,694 --> 00:31:04,612
The one big sticking point tonight

498
00:31:04,696 --> 00:31:08,199
is how much power to give the president
to conduct this war

499
00:31:08,283 --> 00:31:11,494
and to respond
to any future acts of terrorism.

500
00:31:11,578 --> 00:31:15,081
And they sought a super broad
authorization to use military force,

501
00:31:15,164 --> 00:31:16,624
as it's called, an AUMF,

502
00:31:16,708 --> 00:31:22,463
from Congress for a very open-ended
authorization to go after all terrorists,

503
00:31:22,547 --> 00:31:26,467
essentially, all terrorist threats
and to deter all terrorist threats.

504
00:31:26,551 --> 00:31:28,720
[reporter] This draft resolution
from the White House

505
00:31:28,803 --> 00:31:30,805
authorizes use of armed forces

506
00:31:30,889 --> 00:31:32,432
against all those who planned,

507
00:31:32,515 --> 00:31:35,935
authorized, harbored, committed,
or aided in the attacks

508
00:31:36,019 --> 00:31:39,689
and to deter and pre-empt
any future acts of terrorism.

509
00:31:39,772 --> 00:31:42,483
[Dennis Hastert]
Yeas are 420, the nays are one.

510
00:31:42,567 --> 00:31:45,278
This resolution,
joint resolution, is passed.

511
00:31:45,361 --> 00:31:46,362
-[applause]
-[gavel bangs]

512
00:31:49,365 --> 00:31:52,285
[Oona Hathaway] The Constitution
was written to really constrain

513
00:31:52,368 --> 00:31:55,121
the president's authority
to use military force.

514
00:31:55,997 --> 00:31:57,707
{\an8}The idea was
we didn't want to have a king.

515
00:31:57,790 --> 00:32:00,168
{\an8}We didn't want to have
one person who could

516
00:32:00,251 --> 00:32:01,878
{\an8}just launch the country into war.

517
00:32:01,961 --> 00:32:05,798
{\an8}And so the Constitution very carefully
separates out the powers.

518
00:32:05,882 --> 00:32:08,509
It's Congress that gets to declare war.

519
00:32:09,010 --> 00:32:11,804
The 2001 authorization
for use of military force

520
00:32:11,888 --> 00:32:16,935
was enacted not with a lot of time
for deliberation and careful reflection.

521
00:32:18,353 --> 00:32:21,064
[Wolf Blitzer] Would you be willing
to give up some of your liberties

522
00:32:21,147 --> 00:32:23,149
in this country
in order for the government

523
00:32:23,232 --> 00:32:24,734
to crack down on terrorism?

524
00:32:24,817 --> 00:32:27,236
{\an8}66% said yes.

525
00:32:32,033 --> 00:32:34,202
[General Stanley McChrystal]
The AUMF, this 2001 statute

526
00:32:34,285 --> 00:32:36,371
passed a few days after 9/11,

527
00:32:36,454 --> 00:32:41,417
turned out to be the foundation stone
on the next 25 years of war.

528
00:32:43,044 --> 00:32:46,297
The basis for using force abroad
all over the world,

529
00:32:47,090 --> 00:32:49,342
the basis for the Guantanamo detentions,

530
00:32:49,425 --> 00:32:52,804
and the basis in some sense
for the black sites abroad.

531
00:32:52,887 --> 00:32:57,475
It was really the legal foundation
for everything that would come.

532
00:33:03,648 --> 00:33:06,359
[Osama bin Laden in Arabic] We hold
the American government responsible

533
00:33:06,442 --> 00:33:09,612
for its transgressions
on Muslims as a whole,

534
00:33:09,696 --> 00:33:13,157
and for its support of
the regimes in our countries

535
00:33:13,241 --> 00:33:16,619
against the interests
and well-being of the people.

536
00:33:18,579 --> 00:33:22,458
[McChrystal] The 9/11 attacks
had been orchestrated by Al-Qaeda

537
00:33:22,542 --> 00:33:26,254
and led by Osama bin Laden,
and he was in Afghanistan.

538
00:33:26,337 --> 00:33:27,547
[speaking indistinctly]

539
00:33:32,468 --> 00:33:37,223
[McChrystal] He was there as a guest
of the Taliban-controlled government.

540
00:33:42,395 --> 00:33:46,065
The US went to the government
of Afghanistan, the Taliban,

541
00:33:46,149 --> 00:33:49,610
and said, "Give him up, or push him out."

542
00:33:50,153 --> 00:33:53,865
[British reporter] <i>Under mounting</i>
<i>pressure, Afghanistan's Taliban regime</i>

543
00:33:53,948 --> 00:33:57,493
<i>today replied to charges that</i>
<i>the attacks on New York and Washington</i>

544
00:33:57,577 --> 00:33:59,787
<i>were coordinated from its territory.</i>

545
00:34:00,288 --> 00:34:01,414
And they refused.

546
00:34:01,998 --> 00:34:04,667
{\an8}And that meant
that the United States said,

547
00:34:04,751 --> 00:34:06,794
{\an8}"All right, well,
we're gonna come in after him."

548
00:34:18,473 --> 00:34:22,101
[Geraci] We got the call to go forward
and deploy in Afghanistan with my unit.

549
00:34:22,185 --> 00:34:25,354
And being in a unit
that was at the tip of the spear,

550
00:34:25,438 --> 00:34:29,192
that was our job,
to go forth and to accomplish that task.

551
00:34:32,570 --> 00:34:33,905
[firing]

552
00:34:38,951 --> 00:34:42,455
We felt overwhelming support
for our mission during that time.

553
00:34:46,709 --> 00:34:49,170
There was a sense of excitement about it,

554
00:34:50,004 --> 00:34:53,549
a sense of like, fulfilling,
like, what your profession is.

555
00:34:54,842 --> 00:34:57,553
"This is what I'm here for.
This is what we were trained for."

556
00:34:57,637 --> 00:34:59,680
And kind of an aspect of pride

557
00:34:59,764 --> 00:35:02,809
to be in a unit that, you know,
was trained for moments like that.

558
00:35:03,434 --> 00:35:05,478
[people cheering]

559
00:35:05,561 --> 00:35:07,730
[reporter] <i>One by one, the Afghan cities</i>

560
00:35:07,814 --> 00:35:10,691
<i>started to fall</i>
<i>to the US-led opposition forces.</i>

561
00:35:12,443 --> 00:35:15,363
[cheering continues]

562
00:35:25,581 --> 00:35:28,251
[Obaidullah Durani] I was living
in Afghanistan, in Kunar Province.

563
00:35:31,879 --> 00:35:34,632
In Afghanistan,
it was a complete dictatorship.

564
00:35:35,299 --> 00:35:38,928
There was just set-up rules
by the Taliban for the people.

565
00:35:39,929 --> 00:35:43,641
{\an8}I was like, uh, 10 or 11 years old.

566
00:35:43,724 --> 00:35:46,644
My father was listening
to the radio and the news.

567
00:35:47,311 --> 00:35:49,939
That's how we found out about the 9/11.

568
00:35:50,022 --> 00:35:51,858
-[man on radio] <i>…explosion…</i>
-[reporter] <i>Oh my God,</i>

569
00:35:51,941 --> 00:35:54,360
<i>another plane just hit</i>
<i>the World Trade Center.</i>

570
00:35:54,443 --> 00:35:57,071
<i>Another plane.</i>
<i>It was a medium-sized plane.</i>

571
00:35:57,155 --> 00:36:00,199
<i>Unbelievable. It looked like… Oh my…</i>

572
00:36:00,283 --> 00:36:01,367
[indistinct singing]

573
00:36:01,450 --> 00:36:04,287
[Durani] In the time of Taliban,
we were not aware of the war,

574
00:36:04,370 --> 00:36:05,663
what's going on outside.

575
00:36:05,746 --> 00:36:08,249
Like, it was just somewhere
something happened.

576
00:36:10,084 --> 00:36:14,297
But after American arrival in Afghanistan,
then we realized, like,

577
00:36:14,380 --> 00:36:15,965
that something bad happened.

578
00:36:16,048 --> 00:36:19,093
[speaking Afghan language]

579
00:36:21,262 --> 00:36:24,473
[Gopal] When the US invaded in 2001,

580
00:36:24,557 --> 00:36:29,604
the Taliban were viewed by most Afghans
as an extraordinarily repressive regime.

581
00:36:29,687 --> 00:36:33,232
[joyful music playing in background]

582
00:36:33,316 --> 00:36:36,485
And people viewed
the Americans as liberators.

583
00:36:36,569 --> 00:36:39,405
{\an8}And there was a lot of hope,
there was a groundswell of hope

584
00:36:39,488 --> 00:36:42,783
{\an8}that the US would come
and bring development and peace.

585
00:36:46,204 --> 00:36:48,748
By December 2001,
the Taliban had collapsed.

586
00:36:50,208 --> 00:36:51,876
[Durani] One day is Taliban rule.

587
00:36:51,959 --> 00:36:56,088
Another day, there is no rules of Taliban.
Like, everything is brightened up.

588
00:36:56,672 --> 00:36:59,675
Everybody is, like,
"We are liberated right now

589
00:36:59,759 --> 00:37:02,053
and we can live our life as we want."

590
00:37:02,136 --> 00:37:03,471
[instructor speaking indistinctly]

591
00:37:03,554 --> 00:37:08,434
[reporter] <i>Sources tell ABC News there is</i>
<i>evidence Osama bin Laden is still alive,</i>

592
00:37:08,517 --> 00:37:11,938
<i>still on the run, and still in control.</i>

593
00:37:12,021 --> 00:37:15,274
{\an8}That is why we are there.
That is why we are chasing them.

594
00:37:15,358 --> 00:37:16,943
{\an8}That is why we are bombing.

595
00:37:18,110 --> 00:37:21,072
{\an8}That is why we are working
with Afghan forces.

596
00:37:21,155 --> 00:37:23,574
[pilot speaking indistinctly]

597
00:37:24,700 --> 00:37:28,037
It was my dream from childhood
to become a pilot.

598
00:37:28,120 --> 00:37:31,874
And I follow up with my dream
and I became a pilot.

599
00:37:33,876 --> 00:37:37,004
I came to the United States
to become a fighter pilot.

600
00:37:39,090 --> 00:37:41,342
And I moved back to my country

601
00:37:41,926 --> 00:37:44,136
and served in the military
against the Taliban.

602
00:37:51,560 --> 00:37:56,023
Our main goal is to keep our people safe
from the Taliban and the Taliban rules.

603
00:37:56,107 --> 00:37:58,526
{\an8}[glass breaking]

604
00:37:59,443 --> 00:38:00,403
{\an8}[liquid hissing]

605
00:38:02,613 --> 00:38:04,740
Because I have seen them before…

606
00:38:04,824 --> 00:38:06,492
{\an8}[indistinct dialog over radio]

607
00:38:06,575 --> 00:38:09,870
{\an8}…and I didn't want them
to come back in the power.

608
00:38:09,954 --> 00:38:11,330
{\an8}[explosion]

609
00:38:16,294 --> 00:38:19,171
Americans… Like, we were counterparts.

610
00:38:19,255 --> 00:38:21,549
We were, like, allies.

611
00:38:27,096 --> 00:38:29,098
[indistinct speaker on computer]

612
00:38:31,017 --> 00:38:36,063
{\an8}[Mohammad Razvi] In 1978,
I was about seven years old.

613
00:38:36,605 --> 00:38:39,984
{\an8}I came here, and the first thing
I ate was pizza. [laughs]

614
00:38:40,067 --> 00:38:41,694
I was like, "Oh my God."

615
00:38:42,737 --> 00:38:45,823
The place I grew up
is known as Little Pakistan.

616
00:38:46,324 --> 00:38:48,242
It's right in Brooklyn, New York.

617
00:38:48,826 --> 00:38:51,120
My dad opened up the first store,

618
00:38:51,620 --> 00:38:55,207
and then there was a mosque,
and then things started to grow.

619
00:38:56,792 --> 00:39:00,129
You know, immigrants, what do they do?
They work, they work, they work.

620
00:39:03,716 --> 00:39:06,469
And I had a grocery store
across the street, a 99-cent store.

621
00:39:06,552 --> 00:39:08,137
I also had a restaurant.

622
00:39:09,388 --> 00:39:12,308
[laughing]
I was living the American dream.

623
00:39:13,434 --> 00:39:16,812
[indistinct chatter]

624
00:39:16,896 --> 00:39:20,691
Everybody, we all were immigrants
but we are, like, Americans.

625
00:39:22,693 --> 00:39:25,738
I never felt my identity

626
00:39:26,405 --> 00:39:28,574
was negative.

627
00:39:33,954 --> 00:39:36,582
[woman] The whole second tower collapsed.

628
00:39:36,665 --> 00:39:38,250
The whole building is gone.

629
00:39:38,334 --> 00:39:39,502
The whole building.

630
00:39:39,585 --> 00:39:40,544
[sirens blaring]

631
00:39:40,628 --> 00:39:42,296
[Razvi] And the attacks happened.

632
00:39:43,714 --> 00:39:47,176
No, I was just in tears.
I just couldn't believe it at that time.

633
00:39:48,344 --> 00:39:49,553
[exhales]

634
00:39:52,264 --> 00:39:55,893
And these people, what they've just did,
it's just unbelievable.

635
00:39:56,477 --> 00:39:58,729
[woman] Oh, those poor people. Oh God.

636
00:39:59,438 --> 00:40:02,483
[Razvi] After the buildings fell,
the ashes floated

637
00:40:02,566 --> 00:40:04,819
towards right in front of my doorstep.

638
00:40:06,112 --> 00:40:09,240
[crying] When the ashes came,
that was the devastating part…

639
00:40:11,575 --> 00:40:14,120
because that was a moment like,
"Oh my God."

640
00:40:14,203 --> 00:40:16,664
[indistinct dialog]

641
00:40:16,747 --> 00:40:19,458
I was working
actually in this fabric store,

642
00:40:19,542 --> 00:40:21,627
and my Italian paisans
who were next to me,

643
00:40:21,710 --> 00:40:23,379
they're like,
"How could you people do this?"

644
00:40:23,462 --> 00:40:25,423
And I'm like, "These are not my people."

645
00:40:26,465 --> 00:40:28,968
We have three daughters and two boys.

646
00:40:29,051 --> 00:40:31,011
At that time it was just two and two.

647
00:40:31,512 --> 00:40:33,139
I went home and just sat.

648
00:40:33,222 --> 00:40:35,683
I just told them,
we have nothing to do with this.

649
00:40:36,434 --> 00:40:39,478
And when the country's going to war
with this particular group,

650
00:40:39,562 --> 00:40:41,856
what do you think
the local people are going to do?

651
00:40:41,939 --> 00:40:43,190
They're going to go to war on us.

652
00:40:43,274 --> 00:40:45,192
{\an8}[woman] <i>I hope they send all of you back.</i>

653
00:40:45,276 --> 00:40:48,070
{\an8}<i>I hope they kill your people,</i>
<i>innocent people also.</i>

654
00:40:48,154 --> 00:40:50,406
{\an8}<i>Go back to your country.</i>
<i>We don't want you here.</i>

655
00:40:51,449 --> 00:40:53,534
-There's another one.
-[machine] <i>To delete, press one.</i>

656
00:40:53,617 --> 00:40:54,702
[beeps]

657
00:40:54,785 --> 00:40:55,911
[man] <i>Hello, Muslims.</i>

658
00:40:56,704 --> 00:41:00,040
<i>Why'd you kill at least 5,000 people</i>
<i>at the World Trade Center today?</i>

659
00:41:00,708 --> 00:41:02,585
<i>You just woke up</i>
<i>a sleeping giant with the Americans.</i>

660
00:41:02,668 --> 00:41:04,336
<i>We're gonna kick your ****ing ass now.</i>

661
00:41:05,921 --> 00:41:07,006
[indistinct dialog]

662
00:41:07,089 --> 00:41:09,008
We are also Americans

663
00:41:09,091 --> 00:41:12,511
and we are just as much involved
in this tragedy as everybody else.

664
00:41:12,595 --> 00:41:15,347
I'm saying the man
that was the leader of your people

665
00:41:15,431 --> 00:41:17,558
is the man that makes
all the threats against America.

666
00:41:17,641 --> 00:41:22,021
It's very important for us
to not point fingers at a religion.

667
00:41:22,104 --> 00:41:25,357
There are 1.6 billion Muslims
on the face of the earth.

668
00:41:25,441 --> 00:41:29,695
We are being blamed for something that
we have no control or nothing to do with.

669
00:41:29,778 --> 00:41:32,698
Muslims are not terrorists.

670
00:41:32,781 --> 00:41:35,117
Muslims are not terrorists.

671
00:41:35,201 --> 00:41:37,620
All of a sudden, I'm a foreigner.

672
00:41:39,246 --> 00:41:40,331
I'd never felt that.

673
00:41:41,916 --> 00:41:44,210
[reporter] <i>He's felt</i>
<i>under constant surveillance</i>

674
00:41:44,293 --> 00:41:46,670
<i>and is convinced</i>
<i>he's been closely monitored.</i>

675
00:41:46,754 --> 00:41:49,089
[Razvi] Maybe a week after 9/11,

676
00:41:49,173 --> 00:41:51,425
I get a knock on the door, FBI.

677
00:41:51,509 --> 00:41:53,844
"Do you have gatherings
in your basement?" I said yes.

678
00:41:53,928 --> 00:41:56,639
"I have a large family.
We go downstairs and we pray."

679
00:41:56,722 --> 00:41:59,642
Asked further questions.
He goes, "Where do you work?"

680
00:41:59,725 --> 00:42:03,103
I said, "I run the grocery store
and the 99-cent store."

681
00:42:03,187 --> 00:42:06,232
"All right, so maybe we'll stop by
and just see you later then."

682
00:42:06,941 --> 00:42:08,651
If it can happen to me,

683
00:42:09,443 --> 00:42:13,364
could you just imagine if it's happening
to a person who barely speaks English

684
00:42:13,447 --> 00:42:16,534
or who is here
and does not understand the system?

685
00:42:17,117 --> 00:42:18,369
He's going to be terrified.

686
00:42:20,329 --> 00:42:22,748
My oldest daughter,
she was pushed down the steps,

687
00:42:22,831 --> 00:42:26,210
her scarf was pulled off,
and she was cursed at.

688
00:42:26,877 --> 00:42:28,128
I was like, "My God, why'd you…"

689
00:42:28,212 --> 00:42:30,589
She goes, "Dad,
that's exactly why I didn't tell you."

690
00:42:31,215 --> 00:42:33,676
"Because I know you would have
done a huge thing."

691
00:42:33,759 --> 00:42:36,428
It just broke my heart
that she didn't tell me.

692
00:42:36,512 --> 00:42:38,222
But I understood why she didn't tell me

693
00:42:38,305 --> 00:42:41,100
because then she would become
center of attention.

694
00:42:42,768 --> 00:42:47,022
[applause]

695
00:42:50,317 --> 00:42:54,280
{\an8}[Bush] The Homeland Security Act of 2002
takes the next critical steps

696
00:42:54,363 --> 00:42:56,240
{\an8}in defending our country.

697
00:42:57,866 --> 00:43:00,202
[Ackerman] There was never something
called Homeland Security

698
00:43:00,286 --> 00:43:02,413
in the United States before 9/11.

699
00:43:02,496 --> 00:43:04,623
[woman] <i>…most dynamic</i>
<i>federal government organization.</i>

700
00:43:04,707 --> 00:43:06,041
[Ackerman] It sounds off.

701
00:43:06,625 --> 00:43:11,797
It sounds like something that's
a relic of European nationalisms,

702
00:43:11,880 --> 00:43:16,677
as opposed to what had been
a conception of the American way of life,

703
00:43:17,803 --> 00:43:20,097
which is that you can come from anywhere,

704
00:43:20,180 --> 00:43:23,309
and provided that you believe
in these sorts of things

705
00:43:23,392 --> 00:43:27,146
and are willing to work
to live in harmony with one another,

706
00:43:27,229 --> 00:43:29,440
great, we've got another American here.

707
00:43:29,523 --> 00:43:30,774
[applause]

708
00:43:30,858 --> 00:43:32,818
The Department of Homeland Security

709
00:43:32,901 --> 00:43:35,863
was a harbinger
of how all of that was about to change.

710
00:43:37,990 --> 00:43:42,369
Very quickly, it seemed
the focus of the War on Terror

711
00:43:42,453 --> 00:43:45,039
was something much larger,

712
00:43:45,122 --> 00:43:47,833
much more difficult to define.

713
00:43:49,251 --> 00:43:52,880
Now, all of a sudden, the elements of

714
00:43:52,963 --> 00:43:55,883
American immigration policy

715
00:43:55,966 --> 00:43:59,053
had a counterterrorism mandate
behind them.

716
00:43:59,637 --> 00:44:03,724
{\an8}That was the beginnings
of the agencies that would become ICE.

717
00:44:04,350 --> 00:44:05,643
[protesters chanting] Fuck ICE!

718
00:44:05,726 --> 00:44:10,731
{\an8}[Ackerman] The Department of Homeland
Security established conceptually, firmly,

719
00:44:10,814 --> 00:44:13,984
that the War on Terror
was not just happening overseas,

720
00:44:14,068 --> 00:44:15,277
it was happening at home.

721
00:44:15,361 --> 00:44:17,363
[train horn blowing]

722
00:44:23,452 --> 00:44:24,578
[woman] You look so happy.

723
00:44:24,662 --> 00:44:26,705
-[Homer] I like that one.
-[woman] That's a great one.

724
00:44:26,789 --> 00:44:28,415
[Homer] I have a copy
of that in my wallet.

725
00:44:31,669 --> 00:44:36,465
My dad became a pilot at 16 years old.

726
00:44:38,342 --> 00:44:44,098
He got his license and then
he went on to the Air Force Academy.

727
00:44:45,474 --> 00:44:49,144
I've kind of stopped myself
from learning a lot of things about him.

728
00:44:50,396 --> 00:44:54,149
It's still really difficult
for me to know anything

729
00:44:54,233 --> 00:44:55,984
because the more that I do know,

730
00:44:56,068 --> 00:44:59,905
the more that I think about
what I didn't have

731
00:44:59,988 --> 00:45:02,199
and the life I missed out on.

732
00:45:04,201 --> 00:45:05,661
You do look like him.

733
00:45:06,370 --> 00:45:07,788
I know. [laughs sadly]

734
00:45:08,831 --> 00:45:09,832
[sniffles]

735
00:45:10,416 --> 00:45:12,251
I'm not good at looking through photos.

736
00:45:12,960 --> 00:45:14,837
[woman] I know, it's very emotional.

737
00:45:15,421 --> 00:45:16,964
-[Homer] Yeah.
-It's okay.

738
00:45:19,717 --> 00:45:24,304
[Homer] My generation learned about 9/11
through social media,

739
00:45:24,388 --> 00:45:27,975
so Instagram, Twitter, Facebook.

740
00:45:29,101 --> 00:45:32,855
I'm, in a way, like,
some sort of slightly public figure

741
00:45:32,938 --> 00:45:35,149
because you can look me up.

742
00:45:36,567 --> 00:45:40,320
There have been multiple times
where I've been messaged, DM'd about him.

743
00:45:41,238 --> 00:45:44,241
If they're just trying
to get a rise out of me, I don't know.

744
00:45:44,324 --> 00:45:49,913
People will make jokes
about Bush doing 9/11,

745
00:45:49,997 --> 00:45:52,624
jokes about Osama bin Laden,

746
00:45:52,708 --> 00:45:56,378
and it's just become such a meme.

747
00:45:57,296 --> 00:46:00,174
And there's so many conspiracies.

748
00:46:00,257 --> 00:46:03,093
I've seen so many YouTube channels

749
00:46:03,177 --> 00:46:06,138
posting whole videos about conspiracies.

750
00:46:06,221 --> 00:46:09,224
{\an8}Do you think it was possible
that 9/11 was an inside job?

751
00:46:09,308 --> 00:46:13,812
{\an8}Well, I think it's not only possible, but
confirmed that they lied to us about it.

752
00:46:13,896 --> 00:46:16,398
[Homer] Comments that this is all a hoax

753
00:46:16,482 --> 00:46:18,484
or an inside job.

754
00:46:18,567 --> 00:46:20,986
Comments are very insensitive.

755
00:46:24,364 --> 00:46:28,368
And I've had people comment
just, like, disgusting things.

756
00:46:30,454 --> 00:46:32,915
[crying] It just made me realize
how vulnerable I felt

757
00:46:32,998 --> 00:46:36,877
because there's so much
information about this event,

758
00:46:36,960 --> 00:46:40,130
which is something that somebody
could attack me about, basically.

759
00:46:43,509 --> 00:46:45,969
It is kind of just
a historical moment now.

760
00:46:47,137 --> 00:46:51,725
We have a whole new generation
of people who weren't even, like, alive,

761
00:46:51,809 --> 00:46:53,852
or they were young when this happened.

762
00:46:54,561 --> 00:46:56,104
But it's inescapable.

763
00:47:03,111 --> 00:47:06,949
In recent years, I've really been
reflecting on my childhood

764
00:47:07,032 --> 00:47:10,619
and what should have been, kind of.

765
00:47:10,702 --> 00:47:11,829
[cat meows]

766
00:47:13,080 --> 00:47:15,499
I've been trying to find a word for it,

767
00:47:15,582 --> 00:47:19,962
feeling like you were
supposed to have this life

768
00:47:20,629 --> 00:47:25,092
and then, like,
something destroys that entire future.

769
00:47:27,678 --> 00:47:30,305
[disturbing music playing]

770
00:47:31,849 --> 00:47:34,768
[Graff] The shifting
technological landscape

771
00:47:34,852 --> 00:47:38,063
of, you know, the web and social media

772
00:47:38,146 --> 00:47:44,278
supercharges the pre-existing
conspiratorial nature

773
00:47:44,361 --> 00:47:48,907
{\an8}that has long existed
in corners of the United States.

774
00:47:50,576 --> 00:47:56,081
You see 9/11 really collide with

775
00:47:56,164 --> 00:48:00,961
the turbocharging
of conspiracies in America

776
00:48:01,044 --> 00:48:05,966
to launch this entire, you know,
incredibly dark movement

777
00:48:06,049 --> 00:48:08,510
{\an8}that we now call 9/11 Truthers.

778
00:48:09,177 --> 00:48:10,679
Hello, I'm Alex Jones.

779
00:48:10,762 --> 00:48:15,934
{\an8}[Graff] And this poison spreads out across

780
00:48:16,018 --> 00:48:19,855
{\an8}all of the sort of far right and far left

781
00:48:19,938 --> 00:48:23,358
conspiratorial circles in America

782
00:48:23,442 --> 00:48:26,653
and infects our politics.

783
00:48:26,737 --> 00:48:30,949
It affects our national culture
and debate.

784
00:48:32,075 --> 00:48:35,829
And what companies realize very quickly is

785
00:48:36,413 --> 00:48:43,003
{\an8}that the thing that drives engagement
is content that enrages.

786
00:48:43,086 --> 00:48:48,050
[woman] <i>We know that open air fires</i>
<i>cannot burn hot enough to melt steel,</i>

787
00:48:48,133 --> 00:48:51,011
<i>but metal had melted</i>
<i>at the base of the towers.</i>

788
00:48:51,094 --> 00:48:54,431
[Graff] And so you see
these conspiracies spread,

789
00:48:54,514 --> 00:48:57,267
you see political polarization spread.

790
00:48:57,351 --> 00:49:02,022
{\an8}We are facing the rise of
Islamo-Nazism rising all around the world.

791
00:49:03,815 --> 00:49:06,026
[Graff] Other countries figure this out,
like Russia,

792
00:49:06,109 --> 00:49:08,779
begin to sort of use
some of these tools against us

793
00:49:08,862 --> 00:49:13,450
to spread disinformation
and polarizing content online.

794
00:49:14,993 --> 00:49:20,248
There is a very clear through line

795
00:49:20,749 --> 00:49:24,836
that takes us from
the darkest corners of 9/11

796
00:49:24,920 --> 00:49:27,881
to the darkest corners
of our politics today.

797
00:49:28,799 --> 00:49:30,801
[Wendell Clyne]
<i>I guess that went off automatically.</i>

798
00:49:30,884 --> 00:49:33,095
<i>They've evacuated everyone in the hotel.</i>

799
00:49:33,178 --> 00:49:34,680
<i>They've evacuated all employees.</i>

800
00:49:34,763 --> 00:49:36,765
-[Bryant Gumbel] <i>They evacuated the hotel?</i>
<i>-Yes.</i>

801
00:49:36,848 --> 00:49:37,933
-[Gumbel] <i>Immediately?</i>
<i>-Yes.</i>

802
00:49:38,016 --> 00:49:39,559
[Gumbel] <i>Are you</i>
<i>hearing anything, Wendell,</i>

803
00:49:39,643 --> 00:49:41,937
<i>-about what kind of a plane it was?</i>
<i>-No.</i>

804
00:49:42,020 --> 00:49:44,106
-[Gumbel] <i>Or how many were on board?</i>
<i>-No.</i>

805
00:49:44,189 --> 00:49:46,441
[Gumbel] <i>All right. Mr. Clyne,</i>
<i>thank you very much, sir.</i>

806
00:49:46,525 --> 00:49:47,567
<i>I understand Teresa…</i>

807
00:49:47,651 --> 00:49:49,486
[Gopal] Immediately during the attack,

808
00:49:49,569 --> 00:49:52,239
I remember the types of rumors
that we were hearing,

809
00:49:52,322 --> 00:49:54,950
there was just
extraordinary levels of confusion.

810
00:49:56,576 --> 00:49:57,828
We ended up,

811
00:49:57,911 --> 00:50:00,539
meaning undergrads at NYU
who were living near the towers,

812
00:50:00,622 --> 00:50:02,833
ended up being put into a hotel.

813
00:50:02,916 --> 00:50:05,002
And I lived in a hotel
for a couple of months.

814
00:50:05,794 --> 00:50:08,088
At that time, I was studying physics,

815
00:50:09,089 --> 00:50:13,510
but I began to pay attention to
the rest of the world for the first time.

816
00:50:13,593 --> 00:50:15,470
I was young. I was 20.

817
00:50:15,971 --> 00:50:19,391
And I began to follow the War on Terror.

818
00:50:21,852 --> 00:50:24,271
The reason I went
to Afghanistan is because

819
00:50:24,354 --> 00:50:25,772
I'd been following all the news

820
00:50:25,856 --> 00:50:29,026
and felt really dissatisfied with the way
the news was being portrayed,

821
00:50:29,109 --> 00:50:31,737
because I didn't really understand
what was happening.

822
00:50:31,820 --> 00:50:34,823
When I got there,
pretty quickly I began to report,

823
00:50:34,906 --> 00:50:37,617
and I began to see
a different side of the conflict…

824
00:50:40,328 --> 00:50:41,329
in Afghanistan.

825
00:50:41,413 --> 00:50:42,914
You had this weird situation

826
00:50:42,998 --> 00:50:46,168
where you had thousands
of American troops on Afghan soil,

827
00:50:46,251 --> 00:50:47,878
and there was no enemy to fight.

828
00:50:47,961 --> 00:50:52,507
So the way this contradiction was resolved
is that the US began to ally

829
00:50:52,591 --> 00:50:54,384
with warlords around the country.

830
00:50:55,719 --> 00:50:58,096
{\an8}And they said,
"We're here to fight a War on Terror."

831
00:50:58,180 --> 00:51:01,683
{\an8}"We're here to fight bad guys,
and you need to produce us bad guys."

832
00:51:01,767 --> 00:51:03,852
{\an8}"Give us intelligence.
Where are the bad guys?"

833
00:51:03,935 --> 00:51:06,104
-[man] Talib! Talib!
-[man 2 shouting]

834
00:51:07,147 --> 00:51:08,857
[Gopal] They paid these warlords to do so.

835
00:51:08,940 --> 00:51:11,902
So these warlords are now incentivized
to produce intelligence.

836
00:51:11,985 --> 00:51:14,863
So, for example, you have a plot of land,

837
00:51:14,946 --> 00:51:16,406
and I want that land,

838
00:51:16,490 --> 00:51:19,326
I'm going to go tell the Americans
you're a member of Al-Qaeda.

839
00:51:21,244 --> 00:51:23,080
The US troops would
take this intelligence,

840
00:51:23,163 --> 00:51:25,373
they would raid the household,
take the people away.

841
00:51:25,457 --> 00:51:28,627
[in Afghan language] No, I'm not
a member of Al-Qaeda. I'm a student.

842
00:51:28,710 --> 00:51:30,670
[Gopal] This happened
again and again and again.

843
00:51:31,797 --> 00:51:33,590
And the consequence of this was that

844
00:51:33,673 --> 00:51:38,053
whereas there had been only
a groundswell of support for the US,

845
00:51:38,136 --> 00:51:41,723
gradually villagers started
to turn against the Americans,

846
00:51:42,516 --> 00:51:46,436
and no longer saw them as liberators,
but began to see them as occupiers.

847
00:51:47,229 --> 00:51:48,480
[man] Woohoo!

848
00:51:48,563 --> 00:51:51,691
[Gopal] This laid the seeds
for the reconstitution of the Taliban.

849
00:51:54,820 --> 00:51:59,199
{\an8}9/11 and the War on Terror
set in motion a process

850
00:51:59,282 --> 00:52:02,119
{\an8}where trust and faith
in government leaders

851
00:52:02,202 --> 00:52:04,955
was systematically undermined.

852
00:52:05,038 --> 00:52:08,375
We were told that we had to invade Iraq,
for example,

853
00:52:08,458 --> 00:52:12,462
because Saddam Hussein possessed
weapons of mass destruction.

854
00:52:12,963 --> 00:52:15,465
[Bush] We owe it to our children,
our children's children,

855
00:52:15,549 --> 00:52:19,511
to free the world from
weapons of mass destruction.

856
00:52:19,594 --> 00:52:20,887
Weapons of mass destruction.

857
00:52:20,971 --> 00:52:22,514
Weapons of mass destruction?

858
00:52:23,098 --> 00:52:24,766
It turned out not to be true.

859
00:52:26,226 --> 00:52:29,437
And that set in motion
a crisis of confidence

860
00:52:29,521 --> 00:52:35,235
and a divisiveness that has never really
been healed, much less repaired.

861
00:52:35,318 --> 00:52:36,611
[protesters shouting]

862
00:52:36,695 --> 00:52:40,115
We stand for liberation and democracy,
gentlemen!

863
00:52:41,116 --> 00:52:44,995
{\an8}The killing of Osama bin Laden
really, to many people,

864
00:52:45,078 --> 00:52:47,998
{\an8}signified an end to the War on Terror.

865
00:52:48,081 --> 00:52:50,083
{\an8}It was over. It was just mopping up.

866
00:52:50,876 --> 00:52:52,794
[all singing]

867
00:52:52,878 --> 00:52:54,129
[chanting] USA!

868
00:52:54,212 --> 00:52:57,883
[Hoffman] But meanwhile, in Iraq,
a new terrorist group is arising.

869
00:52:57,966 --> 00:52:59,593
[rapid gunfire]

870
00:52:59,676 --> 00:53:02,512
[Gopal] In the chaos
of the US occupation of Iraq,

871
00:53:02,596 --> 00:53:06,766
all sorts of groups start to pop up
to resist the occupiers.

872
00:53:09,561 --> 00:53:12,314
{\an8}One of those groups is Al-Qaeda.

873
00:53:12,397 --> 00:53:14,649
{\an8}Eventually,
through the course of that war,

874
00:53:14,733 --> 00:53:17,986
{\an8}Al-Qaeda radicalizes,
and a section splits off

875
00:53:18,069 --> 00:53:20,113
{\an8}and becomes what we know as ISIS.

876
00:53:21,948 --> 00:53:26,411
{\an8}ISIS becomes one of the most formidable
terrorist organizations in history.

877
00:53:39,883 --> 00:53:43,345
[Khan] On 9/11, I was in English class,

878
00:53:43,428 --> 00:53:49,184
and somebody came into our classroom
and said, "Turn on the TV."

879
00:53:49,267 --> 00:53:50,435
And we did.

880
00:53:51,478 --> 00:53:56,149
And not long after that, the principal
called me and my sister and said,

881
00:53:56,233 --> 00:53:57,651
"You guys can go home."

882
00:53:58,443 --> 00:54:00,612
I think we were
the only Muslims at school,

883
00:54:00,695 --> 00:54:06,826
and they, out of a desire
to be proactive about taking care of us,

884
00:54:07,410 --> 00:54:09,287
let our parents come pick us up.

885
00:54:10,163 --> 00:54:12,207
And as I came of age,

886
00:54:12,290 --> 00:54:16,753
the United States started wars
in Afghanistan and Iraq.

887
00:54:17,462 --> 00:54:21,132
And you saw the real spread
of the cycle of violence.

888
00:54:25,762 --> 00:54:27,597
[Barack Obama]
Happy you're here. Thank you.

889
00:54:28,265 --> 00:54:32,644
[Hoffman] Obama began to rely much more
heavily on targeted assassinations,

890
00:54:32,727 --> 00:54:35,814
using unmanned aircraft, drones,

891
00:54:35,897 --> 00:54:39,651
Predator and Reaper drones
that fired a Hellfire missile.

892
00:54:41,236 --> 00:54:45,198
And would target
not just identified terrorist leaders,

893
00:54:45,282 --> 00:54:49,369
but also undertake
what were called signature strikes.

894
00:54:51,246 --> 00:54:52,956
In other words, even if there wasn't

895
00:54:53,039 --> 00:54:56,293
actual intelligence identifying
the specific individuals,

896
00:54:56,376 --> 00:54:58,336
we would still launch the attack.

897
00:54:58,420 --> 00:55:02,090
And of course, inevitably,
tragedy ensued in the sense that

898
00:55:02,173 --> 00:55:05,427
in some attacks,
innocent civilians were mistakenly killed.

899
00:55:08,054 --> 00:55:09,973
[McChrystal]
When you watch these from afar,

900
00:55:10,056 --> 00:55:12,475
it's almost like
drone strikes on anything.

901
00:55:12,559 --> 00:55:14,436
{\an8}They look like a computer game,

902
00:55:15,061 --> 00:55:16,229
{\an8}and it's an explosion.

903
00:55:16,313 --> 00:55:17,772
{\an8}On the ground, it's very different.

904
00:55:20,900 --> 00:55:24,321
It's a gruesome activity.
People are blown up.

905
00:55:25,030 --> 00:55:26,614
People are burned badly.

906
00:55:29,826 --> 00:55:32,662
[Hathaway]
The Obama administration decided that

907
00:55:32,746 --> 00:55:35,665
they could use
military force against ISIS,

908
00:55:36,249 --> 00:55:38,168
{\an8}even though it didn't exist on 9/11,

909
00:55:38,251 --> 00:55:43,048
{\an8}using authority granted in the 2001
Authorization for Use of Military Force.

910
00:55:45,675 --> 00:55:49,637
It set the stage
for the continued use of military force

911
00:55:49,721 --> 00:55:52,766
under that authority for another decade.

912
00:55:57,020 --> 00:55:59,189
{\an8}[pilot] I took my first flight
when I was 14 years old.

913
00:56:00,357 --> 00:56:04,027
{\an8}And I had my private pilot's license
when I was 16.

914
00:56:04,652 --> 00:56:06,905
{\an8}And I always wanted to be a fighter pilot.

915
00:56:07,947 --> 00:56:12,702
When I was a kid, I burned through
two VHS copies of <i>Top Gun.</i>

916
00:56:13,328 --> 00:56:15,789
After 9/11,
I went to the Marine Corps recruiter,

917
00:56:15,872 --> 00:56:17,665
and he said,
"How many pull-ups can you do?"

918
00:56:21,044 --> 00:56:24,297
The mission we were supporting
was Operation Inherent Resolve.

919
00:56:25,006 --> 00:56:27,717
It encompassed Iraq and Syria.

920
00:56:31,137 --> 00:56:34,474
Our job was to provide air support

921
00:56:34,557 --> 00:56:37,936
either through reconnaissance
or direct action,

922
00:56:38,019 --> 00:56:39,938
dropping bombs, strikes, things like that,

923
00:56:40,688 --> 00:56:41,856
against ISIS.

924
00:56:44,234 --> 00:56:48,613
I would like my identity
to remain anonymous just because

925
00:56:49,197 --> 00:56:52,784
I'm sure that there's people out there
that would want to seek retribution.

926
00:56:53,660 --> 00:56:55,328
I wish we didn't have to do it.

927
00:56:56,413 --> 00:56:57,622
I loved my job.

928
00:56:58,415 --> 00:56:59,958
I loved killing ISIS.

929
00:57:01,334 --> 00:57:05,296
But it's not lost on me
of the gravity of what we were doing.

930
00:57:06,339 --> 00:57:09,259
I'd rather be back in San Diego

931
00:57:09,342 --> 00:57:11,344
eating fish tacos on the beach.

932
00:57:11,428 --> 00:57:14,055
But this is what I signed up for.
This is what they're paying me to do.

933
00:57:14,139 --> 00:57:16,015
This is what they're trusting me to do.

934
00:57:16,099 --> 00:57:17,934
So I'm going to do it.

935
00:57:28,653 --> 00:57:33,741
[Khan] I remember so well these
really impossible statistics coming out.

936
00:57:33,825 --> 00:57:38,621
The United States saying that they'd
killed as many as 25,000 ISIS fighters.

937
00:57:38,705 --> 00:57:44,002
And at the same time,
only admitting 21 or 24 civilian deaths.

938
00:57:44,502 --> 00:57:45,879
That's just not possible.

939
00:57:49,132 --> 00:57:54,637
I began trying to get on the ground and
investigate civilian casualties myself.

940
00:57:56,973 --> 00:57:58,892
{\an8}You know, if you take
the government's data

941
00:57:58,975 --> 00:58:02,937
{\an8}about what percentage that they admitted
civilian deaths were occurring,

942
00:58:03,021 --> 00:58:05,106
{\an8}it would be less than
1% of all these strikes

943
00:58:05,190 --> 00:58:06,983
{\an8}were resulting in a civilian death.

944
00:58:08,401 --> 00:58:12,864
{\an8}But I spent a lot of time
going on the ground,

945
00:58:12,947 --> 00:58:14,866
going to a sample of these airstrikes,

946
00:58:14,949 --> 00:58:17,243
and what I found was that

947
00:58:17,327 --> 00:58:21,289
one in five strikes
was resulting in a civilian death.

948
00:58:21,372 --> 00:58:25,502
That's not two times higher, three times
higher than what the government thought.

949
00:58:25,585 --> 00:58:27,712
That was 31 times higher.

950
00:58:31,591 --> 00:58:33,843
And people were so inviting.

951
00:58:34,636 --> 00:58:35,887
They were so welcoming.

952
00:58:35,970 --> 00:58:39,307
I can't remember anyone
who said no to talking to me.

953
00:58:40,391 --> 00:58:43,228
For so many of these civilians,
there had been a belief

954
00:58:43,311 --> 00:58:46,940
that the Americans can be precise.
They can be surgical.

955
00:58:47,023 --> 00:58:49,442
I remember one man pointing at a teacup,

956
00:58:49,526 --> 00:58:51,236
holding his teacup, and saying,

957
00:58:51,319 --> 00:58:53,905
"The Americans could just hit
this teacup if they wanted."

958
00:58:53,988 --> 00:58:55,156
That was his belief.

959
00:58:56,199 --> 00:58:57,951
"And look at what they did to my family."

960
00:59:01,120 --> 00:59:02,247
Over and over,

961
00:59:02,747 --> 00:59:05,458
I heard from parents
who would tell me about

962
00:59:05,542 --> 00:59:07,126
the way their daughter laughed,

963
00:59:07,919 --> 00:59:12,298
or would show me a photo
of her holding, like, a firework,

964
00:59:12,382 --> 00:59:13,883
the night before she was killed.

965
00:59:13,967 --> 00:59:16,010
Like her vibrancy, her youth,

966
00:59:16,511 --> 00:59:19,305
that particular loss
of outliving your child.

967
00:59:20,515 --> 00:59:22,141
That always stays with me.

968
00:59:23,476 --> 00:59:25,103
Like I don't know how…

969
00:59:27,355 --> 00:59:29,107
people move on after that.

970
00:59:34,320 --> 00:59:36,364
{\an8}[Stephen Wertheim]
By the time Obama left office,

971
00:59:36,447 --> 00:59:40,451
{\an8}the United States was
fighting terrorism in some form

972
00:59:40,535 --> 00:59:42,870
{\an8}in approximately 13 countries.

973
00:59:44,205 --> 00:59:48,209
The phrase "endless war"
and "forever war,"

974
00:59:48,293 --> 00:59:50,920
become more widely used.

975
00:59:53,631 --> 00:59:54,549
[siren wailing]

976
00:59:54,632 --> 00:59:57,635
{\an8}Obama, even as he is the drone president,

977
00:59:57,719 --> 01:00:00,388
{\an8}conducting these drone strikes
on a routine basis

978
01:00:00,471 --> 01:00:02,348
{\an8}over more and more of the Earth,

979
01:00:02,890 --> 01:00:04,350
{\an8}is saying out loud, you know,

980
01:00:04,434 --> 01:00:09,314
{\an8}how do we know if we're not creating
more terrorists than we're killing?

981
01:00:12,734 --> 01:00:14,819
[man] We have a problem in this country.

982
01:00:14,902 --> 01:00:15,903
It's called Muslims.

983
01:00:16,487 --> 01:00:18,906
We know our current president is one.

984
01:00:18,990 --> 01:00:21,117
-Right.
-You know he's not even an American.

985
01:00:21,200 --> 01:00:23,494
-We need this question.
-[man] Birth certificate, man.

986
01:00:23,578 --> 01:00:29,542
{\an8}But anyway, we have training camps
growing where they want to kill us.

987
01:00:30,376 --> 01:00:32,420
That's my question.
When can we get rid of 'em?

988
01:00:32,503 --> 01:00:34,422
We're gonna be looking at a lot of things.

989
01:00:34,505 --> 01:00:36,507
And you know,
a lot of people are saying that

990
01:00:36,591 --> 01:00:39,344
and a lot of people are saying
that bad things are happening out there.

991
01:00:39,427 --> 01:00:41,471
[Ackerman]
Donald Trump is not a subtle man

992
01:00:41,554 --> 01:00:44,015
but understands subtext extremely well.

993
01:00:44,098 --> 01:00:45,767
[crowd chanting] Trump! Trump!

994
01:00:45,850 --> 01:00:50,480
[Ackerman] What he saw very early on
was that the War on Terror

995
01:00:50,563 --> 01:00:52,982
was a pathway to power

996
01:00:53,066 --> 01:00:56,152
for inflamed nativist elements

997
01:00:56,235 --> 01:00:59,989
{\an8}that felt the country was further
and further slipping away from them.

998
01:01:00,573 --> 01:01:04,661
Donald J. Trump is calling for
a total and complete shutdown

999
01:01:04,744 --> 01:01:08,164
of Muslims entering
the United States. Until…

1000
01:01:08,247 --> 01:01:13,169
[Ackerman] Trump's movement
recognized that there was

1001
01:01:13,252 --> 01:01:16,964
still a tremendous amount
of political potency to be had

1002
01:01:17,048 --> 01:01:18,841
from the War on Terror.

1003
01:01:18,925 --> 01:01:21,969
I think Islam… hates us.

1004
01:01:22,053 --> 01:01:24,972
Build the wall! Build the wall!

1005
01:01:25,056 --> 01:01:27,392
I want surveillance of certain mosques.

1006
01:01:27,475 --> 01:01:28,518
[siren blips]

1007
01:01:29,060 --> 01:01:33,690
[Graff] The border becomes
this dominant topic of conversation

1008
01:01:33,773 --> 01:01:37,276
all out of proportion
to the actual problems at the border

1009
01:01:37,360 --> 01:01:39,404
in the 2016 campaign.

1010
01:01:39,487 --> 01:01:41,781
{\an8}Folks, brick by brick,

1011
01:01:41,864 --> 01:01:44,283
{\an8}that wall is going to get built.

1012
01:01:44,826 --> 01:01:49,831
We're gonna have a situation on our border
that isn't mired in violence and chaos.

1013
01:01:49,914 --> 01:01:52,750
-…The left. Who is on your list?
-Will you shut up, man?

1014
01:01:52,834 --> 01:01:56,587
{\an8}[Hoffman] Four years later,
in the 2020 presidential campaign,

1015
01:01:57,213 --> 01:01:59,716
I think arguably there was only one issue

1016
01:01:59,799 --> 01:02:03,761
that President Trump and
presidential candidate Biden agreed on,

1017
01:02:04,679 --> 01:02:07,140
and that was ending the endless wars.

1018
01:02:07,974 --> 01:02:09,851
[Trump] Afghanistan, how about that?

1019
01:02:10,643 --> 01:02:13,813
Where, by the way, I started the process,
all the troops are coming back home,

1020
01:02:13,896 --> 01:02:15,314
they couldn't stop the process.

1021
01:02:15,398 --> 01:02:16,399
[cheering]

1022
01:02:17,066 --> 01:02:19,402
{\an8}[reporter] <i>Militants captured</i>
<i>the pilot in December</i>

1023
01:02:19,485 --> 01:02:22,029
{\an8}<i>when his F-16 crashed in Syria.</i>

1024
01:02:22,113 --> 01:02:23,197
<i>This video may be…</i>

1025
01:02:23,281 --> 01:02:26,743
[Wilson] I had just
turned three years old on 9/11.

1026
01:02:26,826 --> 01:02:30,621
My birthday was nine days
before everything went down.

1027
01:02:31,956 --> 01:02:34,667
At least for me personally,
joining the Marine Corps

1028
01:02:34,751 --> 01:02:38,254
was to fight ISIS
because that is kind of what I saw.

1029
01:02:38,337 --> 01:02:41,174
{\an8}[reporter] <i>…by the Islamic State</i>
<i>extremist group.</i>

1030
01:02:41,257 --> 01:02:45,595
<i>A video posted online today shows</i>
<i>the captors burning the man alive, while…</i>

1031
01:02:45,678 --> 01:02:49,182
[Wilson] It was like, I'm pissed
and I want to go do something about it.

1032
01:02:49,265 --> 01:02:51,142
It wasn't 9/11.

1033
01:02:51,726 --> 01:02:55,271
I think that's a big shift
between the early GWOT war

1034
01:02:55,855 --> 01:02:58,775
{\an8}compared to mostly my generation.

1035
01:02:59,817 --> 01:03:02,862
{\an8}And I remember being like,
I want to be a part of that,

1036
01:03:02,945 --> 01:03:04,155
of trying to stop that.

1037
01:03:07,033 --> 01:03:13,539
[indistinct shouting in background]

1038
01:03:13,623 --> 01:03:19,003
[Sanchez] I shipped to boot camp
August 26 of 2017.

1039
01:03:20,129 --> 01:03:22,381
And then graduated boot camp

1040
01:03:22,465 --> 01:03:26,385
in October or early November.

1041
01:03:26,469 --> 01:03:29,138
I had always heard that
Marines were the killers.

1042
01:03:29,889 --> 01:03:33,893
I wanted to go to Afghanistan
or Iraq and kill terrorists.

1043
01:03:33,976 --> 01:03:35,937
That was my dream as a kid.

1044
01:03:40,608 --> 01:03:43,945
{\an8}So that was the same unit
as Smoak and Wyatt.

1045
01:03:47,990 --> 01:03:51,744
{\an8}Wyatt, he was a very serious guy. [laughs]

1046
01:03:52,745 --> 01:03:54,372
He would always look annoyed.

1047
01:03:55,498 --> 01:03:57,124
[exhales]

1048
01:03:57,708 --> 01:03:59,126
I remember always seeing him

1049
01:03:59,210 --> 01:04:01,921
and he would always
look annoyed or pissed or something.

1050
01:04:02,004 --> 01:04:04,340
But he was a good guy.
I mean, I liked him a lot.

1051
01:04:10,555 --> 01:04:12,265
[Hoffman] In April 2021,

1052
01:04:12,348 --> 01:04:15,852
{\an8}President Biden announced that
the United States would be withdrawing

1053
01:04:15,935 --> 01:04:19,689
{\an8}from Afghanistan by September 11, 2021.

1054
01:04:19,772 --> 01:04:20,773
{\an8}We went to Afghanistan

1055
01:04:20,857 --> 01:04:24,819
{\an8}because of a horrific attack
that happened 20 years ago.

1056
01:04:25,486 --> 01:04:28,906
[reporter] <i>President essentially defending</i>
<i>a position he has held for a while now,</i>

1057
01:04:28,990 --> 01:04:32,410
<i>which is that there was</i>
<i>no military option left in Afghanistan.</i>

1058
01:04:32,493 --> 01:04:36,831
<i>And he said he didn't want</i>
<i>to send another generation, essentially,</i>

1059
01:04:36,914 --> 01:04:41,419
<i>of young men and women to go fight</i>
<i>an unwinnable war, in his view.</i>

1060
01:04:43,212 --> 01:04:46,132
[reporter 2] <i>From the moment</i>
<i>US troops started pulling back,</i>

1061
01:04:46,215 --> 01:04:48,593
<i>the Taliban went on the offensive.</i>

1062
01:04:50,052 --> 01:04:51,345
[reporter 3] <i>In a matter of days,</i>

1063
01:04:51,429 --> 01:04:54,348
<i>the Taliban has</i>
<i>blitzed across Afghanistan,</i>

1064
01:04:54,432 --> 01:04:56,767
<i>taken over city after city.</i>

1065
01:04:59,353 --> 01:05:04,025
[Smoak] We were sitting in the chow hall
eating probably lunch or dinner,

1066
01:05:04,108 --> 01:05:05,985
and I was watching it on TV,

1067
01:05:06,068 --> 01:05:08,779
{\an8}and all the provinces were falling.

1068
01:05:10,156 --> 01:05:14,368
{\an8}They were changing them red on the TV
when the Taliban would take them.

1069
01:05:14,452 --> 01:05:17,413
{\an8}And it was all just surrounding,
headed towards Kabul.

1070
01:05:17,496 --> 01:05:19,248
{\an8}My thoughts were,

1071
01:05:19,332 --> 01:05:22,376
{\an8}what the hell
have we been doing for 20 years?

1072
01:05:22,960 --> 01:05:26,589
Why were all the lives wasted,
pretty much,

1073
01:05:26,672 --> 01:05:28,382
if we're just going to turn around

1074
01:05:28,466 --> 01:05:30,676
and they're just going to
take it right back over?

1075
01:05:30,760 --> 01:05:33,638
{\an8}[siren blaring]

1076
01:05:33,721 --> 01:05:37,683
{\an8}[man speaking Afghan language]

1077
01:05:37,767 --> 01:05:40,353
{\an8}You see all the Talibans on the road.

1078
01:05:40,436 --> 01:05:41,729
{\an8}This is scary.

1079
01:05:42,688 --> 01:05:43,856
Think about it.

1080
01:05:43,940 --> 01:05:45,566
They're in the control.

1081
01:05:46,108 --> 01:05:47,902
Someone with my history,

1082
01:05:47,985 --> 01:05:49,320
through all the records,

1083
01:05:49,403 --> 01:05:51,614
like, the place where we have bombed,

1084
01:05:51,697 --> 01:05:54,033
it's still in the computers with them.

1085
01:05:54,116 --> 01:05:56,202
My biometric data is with them.

1086
01:05:56,285 --> 01:05:58,287
Everybody's biometric data is with them.

1087
01:05:59,246 --> 01:06:01,290
There is no government, nothing.

1088
01:06:01,374 --> 01:06:04,126
It's like if you are living
in a bright place

1089
01:06:04,210 --> 01:06:06,087
and suddenly the light goes out…

1090
01:06:06,170 --> 01:06:07,129
[man] <i>Allahu Akbar!</i>

1091
01:06:07,213 --> 01:06:09,632
…you're just in the middle of chaos.

1092
01:06:10,841 --> 01:06:14,053
[Hoffman] Hamid Karzai
International Airport was just thronged

1093
01:06:14,136 --> 01:06:16,430
with Afghans desperate to flee.

1094
01:06:16,514 --> 01:06:19,433
[people clamoring]

1095
01:06:19,517 --> 01:06:22,770
People who had worked with
the United States military,

1096
01:06:22,853 --> 01:06:24,772
with the United States government,

1097
01:06:25,898 --> 01:06:31,737
office clerks, translators,
mechanics, cleaning personnel,

1098
01:06:31,821 --> 01:06:36,826
or who worked for any United States
organization, even for US NGOs.

1099
01:06:36,909 --> 01:06:39,495
[person screaming]

1100
01:06:41,080 --> 01:06:44,709
{\an8}They feared that
they'd be singled out for vengeance.

1101
01:06:50,256 --> 01:06:54,218
[reporter] <i>Evacuations are proceeding</i>
<i>slowly with thousands of Americans still</i>

1102
01:06:54,301 --> 01:06:56,429
<i>wanting and waiting to get out.</i>

1103
01:06:56,512 --> 01:06:58,639
[reporter 2] <i>It could be</i>
<i>the largest evacuation</i>

1104
01:06:58,723 --> 01:07:01,851
<i>since the fall of Saigon 46 years ago.</i>

1105
01:07:02,643 --> 01:07:04,353
[Nguyen] Seeing the fall of Kabul,

1106
01:07:05,187 --> 01:07:06,689
it was striking but also painful

1107
01:07:06,772 --> 01:07:07,982
from a family perspective.

1108
01:07:09,984 --> 01:07:13,696
{\an8}My mom told me about when
the South Vietnamese government fell

1109
01:07:13,779 --> 01:07:15,448
{\an8}to the communist north.

1110
01:07:16,365 --> 01:07:20,786
{\an8}You know, the similarity is just like
the Taliban taking over the capital city.

1111
01:07:22,872 --> 01:07:25,708
And just people trying to escape

1112
01:07:25,791 --> 01:07:30,212
from what they know that would be
a catastrophic situation.

1113
01:07:30,296 --> 01:07:34,383
It has very similar emotion,
but also just the imagery itself.

1114
01:07:36,802 --> 01:07:38,763
[people clamoring]

1115
01:07:38,846 --> 01:07:41,098
History somehow repeats itself.

1116
01:07:44,310 --> 01:07:45,853
Sometimes there's no words.

1117
01:07:45,936 --> 01:07:48,647
[people shouting and clamoring]

1118
01:07:48,731 --> 01:07:50,983
[reporter] <i>Many of them,</i>
<i>of course, have legitimate claims</i>

1119
01:07:51,067 --> 01:07:53,319
<i>they are eligible for flights,</i>

1120
01:07:53,402 --> 01:07:55,112
<i>but the Taliban</i>
<i>aren't letting them through,</i>

1121
01:07:55,196 --> 01:07:58,115
<i>and their response on the ground</i>
<i>is brutal.</i>

1122
01:07:58,199 --> 01:07:59,950
[rapid gunfire]

1123
01:08:00,034 --> 01:08:04,080
<i>They're firing automatic weapons</i>
<i>over the heads of the crowd repeatedly.</i>

1124
01:08:04,163 --> 01:08:05,289
[gunfire]

1125
01:08:05,372 --> 01:08:07,458
[people shouting and clamoring]

1126
01:08:07,541 --> 01:08:09,251
<i>They're using sticks to beat people.</i>

1127
01:08:09,335 --> 01:08:11,712
<i>They're pointing guns in people's faces.</i>

1128
01:08:14,757 --> 01:08:17,009
[Wilson] That's when
we started to hear rumors.

1129
01:08:18,427 --> 01:08:21,305
It's like, "Oh shit, what's happening?
What's gonna happen?"

1130
01:08:21,388 --> 01:08:24,850
And then it comes down that,
"Hey, you're going."

1131
01:08:25,518 --> 01:08:29,063
And we're like, "Oh shit, here we go.
This is what we want."

1132
01:08:29,688 --> 01:08:32,983
Just feeling like,
finally a real world mission.

1133
01:08:33,526 --> 01:08:34,902
[reporter] <i>US President Joe Biden</i>

1134
01:08:34,985 --> 01:08:37,863
<i>authorized the deployment</i>
<i>of roughly 5,000 troops</i>

1135
01:08:37,947 --> 01:08:39,907
<i>to support the drawdown.</i>

1136
01:08:41,867 --> 01:08:44,120
[Wilson] And it's like,
all right, gear check.

1137
01:08:44,203 --> 01:08:46,997
Are the guys ready to go?
Who's going? Who's not going?

1138
01:08:48,040 --> 01:08:51,252
[Sanchez] You start loading ammo.
Everybody's loading ammo, getting ready.

1139
01:08:51,335 --> 01:08:52,795
[soldier] Everyone up! Target gun!

1140
01:08:52,878 --> 01:08:54,713
-[Sanchez] Everybody's excited.
-[rapid fire]

1141
01:08:54,797 --> 01:08:56,674
This was, like, the dream.

1142
01:08:57,550 --> 01:09:02,096
The dream place to go for a Marine Corps
infantryman is Afghanistan, Iraq.

1143
01:09:02,179 --> 01:09:03,264
[rapid fire continues]

1144
01:09:03,347 --> 01:09:06,475
[Smoak] I was kind of talking
to my guys about before we go, you know,

1145
01:09:06,559 --> 01:09:07,935
whatever God you believe in,

1146
01:09:08,018 --> 01:09:10,354
you need to get right with him
because anything can happen.

1147
01:09:11,522 --> 01:09:15,067
We got told that we were going in
for a noncombative evacuation.

1148
01:09:16,360 --> 01:09:20,072
We were going to go get US citizens,
Afghans with the proper credentials.

1149
01:09:20,156 --> 01:09:21,949
We were gonna get them out of the country.

1150
01:09:24,326 --> 01:09:26,203
Operation Allies Refuge.

1151
01:09:28,831 --> 01:09:30,416
We flew out of Jordan,

1152
01:09:30,499 --> 01:09:31,375
landed in Kuwait.

1153
01:09:31,458 --> 01:09:33,836
We were in Kuwait 36 hours maybe.

1154
01:09:34,336 --> 01:09:37,173
[Smoak] And then we flew
from Kuwait into Afghanistan

1155
01:09:37,256 --> 01:09:39,800
and landed at night at Kabul Airport.

1156
01:09:40,342 --> 01:09:43,971
You could hear gunshots in the distance
and then you'd see tracers fly.

1157
01:09:44,054 --> 01:09:44,889
[gunfire]

1158
01:09:44,972 --> 01:09:47,766
Made sure all my guys had all
their stuff on, everything was good.

1159
01:09:48,934 --> 01:09:51,020
We get told we're going to Abbey Gate.

1160
01:09:54,148 --> 01:09:56,358
I'm like, "Hell yeah, okay,
so we're going to Abbey Gate."

1161
01:09:56,442 --> 01:09:58,444
Nobody knew what Abbey Gate was.

1162
01:09:58,527 --> 01:10:00,571
I'm like, "Here we fucking go, dudes."

1163
01:10:00,654 --> 01:10:02,156
{\an8}[indistinct chatter]

1164
01:10:02,239 --> 01:10:03,073
{\an8}[dog barks]

1165
01:10:06,535 --> 01:10:08,537
[speaking indistinctly]

1166
01:10:17,755 --> 01:10:19,340
[Wilson] And we get there.

1167
01:10:19,423 --> 01:10:22,301
I remember looking around like,
"What the fuck is this?"

1168
01:10:24,970 --> 01:10:27,306
[all clamoring]

1169
01:10:33,437 --> 01:10:37,066
[shouting]

1170
01:10:37,900 --> 01:10:40,986
[Sanchez] I remember seeing, like,
so many people in front of this gate.

1171
01:10:41,070 --> 01:10:42,112
[shouting]

1172
01:10:42,196 --> 01:10:44,490
[Wilson] It's just a wall of humans.

1173
01:10:55,000 --> 01:10:58,963
[Hoffman] We deployed these young Marines
who were trained, of course, in combat,

1174
01:10:59,046 --> 01:11:00,297
every Marine is a rifleman,

1175
01:11:00,381 --> 01:11:03,467
but they're doing things they've
never been prepared or trained for.

1176
01:11:03,550 --> 01:11:05,636
Crowd control, for example.

1177
01:11:15,604 --> 01:11:17,982
Interpreting official pieces of paper

1178
01:11:18,065 --> 01:11:20,734
that may be written in
one of the Afghan dialects,

1179
01:11:20,818 --> 01:11:22,236
not necessarily in English.

1180
01:11:24,029 --> 01:11:25,281
[soldier] Hey, hey!

1181
01:11:25,364 --> 01:11:27,741
[Hoffman] This is a highly unstable,

1182
01:11:27,825 --> 01:11:29,827
highly volatile situation.

1183
01:11:35,457 --> 01:11:38,168
[Smoak] This was
nearly 20 years after 9/11.

1184
01:11:39,920 --> 01:11:42,047
And what started on 9/11

1185
01:11:42,131 --> 01:11:44,383
ended right here at Abbey Gate.

1186
01:11:46,135 --> 01:11:49,888
We were told we were to treat the Taliban
like they were our allies.

1187
01:11:50,389 --> 01:11:54,893
We're there to work together
to get these people out of Afghanistan.

1188
01:11:56,729 --> 01:11:57,688
That is

1189
01:11:59,064 --> 01:12:01,483
crazy to hear.

1190
01:12:01,567 --> 01:12:02,693
[men yelling]

1191
01:12:02,776 --> 01:12:05,863
Especially for, like,
our platoon sergeants

1192
01:12:05,946 --> 01:12:11,410
who had been in for almost 20 years
and had been at the height of the war

1193
01:12:11,493 --> 01:12:13,203
and been there, done that.

1194
01:12:13,871 --> 01:12:18,125
The people who killed my friends
10 years ago, they're my ally now.

1195
01:12:18,792 --> 01:12:22,004
It was very, very surreal to hear that.

1196
01:12:24,340 --> 01:12:27,634
[pilot] Abbey Gate was a microcosm of

1197
01:12:28,927 --> 01:12:31,096
the 20-plus year war in the Middle East.

1198
01:12:32,264 --> 01:12:34,350
Watching Afghanistan crumble,

1199
01:12:34,433 --> 01:12:38,228
I don't know how any person
can see Afghanistan, Iraq,

1200
01:12:38,312 --> 01:12:40,189
anything other than a failure.

1201
01:12:41,190 --> 01:12:46,278
A lot of these people did not have
a US passport, a green card, a visa.

1202
01:12:46,362 --> 01:12:48,572
They didn't have anything.
They just wanted to leave.

1203
01:12:48,655 --> 01:12:50,574
They were just scared.

1204
01:12:51,992 --> 01:12:55,454
[horns honking]

1205
01:12:55,537 --> 01:12:59,083
There was chaos inside
and outside of the city.

1206
01:13:00,584 --> 01:13:04,254
And going into the airport,
the gates are closed,

1207
01:13:04,338 --> 01:13:08,092
and they're not letting people
go inside because there were,

1208
01:13:08,175 --> 01:13:10,094
like, thousands of people.

1209
01:13:11,303 --> 01:13:12,930
I was holding the kids.

1210
01:13:13,722 --> 01:13:18,018
My daughter was three months old,
and my son was two and a half.

1211
01:13:18,102 --> 01:13:20,062
[people clamoring]

1212
01:13:20,145 --> 01:13:23,273
[Wilson] We didn't have anywhere to
send them back out if they were rejected.

1213
01:13:24,316 --> 01:13:28,195
So we made a hole in a chain-link fence,
and we called it the Freedom Gate.

1214
01:13:28,278 --> 01:13:32,866
And if you couldn't make it,
I'm sorry, but through there you go.

1215
01:13:34,034 --> 01:13:35,661
-[shot firing]
-[people screaming]

1216
01:13:38,205 --> 01:13:40,958
[Smoak] The Taliban would be
on the other side of this,

1217
01:13:41,041 --> 01:13:44,253
and if they did not get through,
they would beat 'em,

1218
01:13:45,003 --> 01:13:48,465
shoot 'em, and then
it just kind of sucked because,

1219
01:13:49,341 --> 01:13:53,262
you know, I'm the one sitting there,
and I know what's going to happen to 'em,

1220
01:13:53,345 --> 01:13:55,764
but I-I… can't do nothing

1221
01:13:56,348 --> 01:13:58,559
if they didn't have the right paperwork,

1222
01:13:59,309 --> 01:14:05,149
which is a pretty crazy responsibility
for a 23-year-old.

1223
01:14:06,942 --> 01:14:10,821
I would like to think that
I didn't lose compassion for people,

1224
01:14:11,947 --> 01:14:13,699
but you definitely become numb.

1225
01:14:15,117 --> 01:14:18,662
[somber music playing]

1226
01:14:18,745 --> 01:14:21,999
Word was getting out that
we were going to close all the gates.

1227
01:14:22,082 --> 01:14:24,460
There was a lot of desperation,

1228
01:14:24,543 --> 01:14:26,462
people giving their kids up.

1229
01:14:33,719 --> 01:14:35,637
[Sanchez] Some of those people
had babies on them,

1230
01:14:35,721 --> 01:14:39,725
so they thought the best option
was probably, like, throw the babies over.

1231
01:14:43,687 --> 01:14:45,189
[soldier speaking indistinctly]

1232
01:14:46,231 --> 01:14:50,027
It was very surreal to see,
like, because I have kids,

1233
01:14:51,278 --> 01:14:55,032
and it was hard to think about
giving one of them up like that.

1234
01:14:55,115 --> 01:14:56,575
[laughs sadly] Yeah.

1235
01:15:00,913 --> 01:15:02,915
[children crying]

1236
01:15:03,874 --> 01:15:06,126
[Wilson] I had a picture of my daughter
in my Kevlar.

1237
01:15:07,252 --> 01:15:09,463
Four days after I deployed
on my first deployment

1238
01:15:09,546 --> 01:15:11,215
is when my oldest was born.

1239
01:15:13,425 --> 01:15:15,385
My platoon sergeant also has kids,

1240
01:15:15,469 --> 01:15:16,970
and I don't know what it was,

1241
01:15:17,054 --> 01:15:19,640
but we kind of just bumped
into each other in passing,

1242
01:15:21,350 --> 01:15:22,976
and I ended up just breaking down.

1243
01:15:23,060 --> 01:15:25,103
I lost it right then and there.

1244
01:15:29,233 --> 01:15:30,567
The new generation.

1245
01:15:34,988 --> 01:15:37,574
My wife was sick at the time.

1246
01:15:37,658 --> 01:15:38,575
She was weak.

1247
01:15:38,659 --> 01:15:40,744
She was not able to walk properly.

1248
01:15:41,453 --> 01:15:45,123
My daughter was so small,
and the American Marine, he said,

1249
01:15:45,207 --> 01:15:47,042
"Give me your daughter,
I will get her inside,

1250
01:15:47,125 --> 01:15:48,835
and you guys can come through the gate."

1251
01:15:50,087 --> 01:15:52,172
My wife was left behind…

1252
01:15:52,256 --> 01:15:53,715
[shouting in Afghan language]

1253
01:15:54,716 --> 01:15:56,677
…and they shut the gate after her.

1254
01:15:57,427 --> 01:15:59,596
And she's still back in Afghanistan.

1255
01:16:02,474 --> 01:16:03,892
That's terrible.

1256
01:16:06,395 --> 01:16:09,147
I was told that we will evacuate her.

1257
01:16:09,815 --> 01:16:11,858
I have not seen anything.

1258
01:16:15,612 --> 01:16:16,947
I was happy.

1259
01:16:18,824 --> 01:16:20,617
Suddenly you lose everything.

1260
01:16:23,912 --> 01:16:25,664
You have a happy life,

1261
01:16:27,374 --> 01:16:29,585
and you lose it in one day.

1262
01:16:34,298 --> 01:16:36,300
{\an8}[Hoffman] To compound matters,

1263
01:16:36,383 --> 01:16:39,720
intelligence was received that ISIS-K…

1264
01:16:40,387 --> 01:16:44,474
{\an8}So in other words,
the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria,

1265
01:16:44,558 --> 01:16:48,395
had a branch in Afghanistan
called ISIS-Khorasan.

1266
01:16:50,606 --> 01:16:54,067
Khorasan is the ancient name
for that part of the region.

1267
01:16:54,151 --> 01:16:56,069
ISIS-K was planning an attack.

1268
01:17:01,366 --> 01:17:05,454
[Sanchez] We got told that
it was going to be a suicide bomber

1269
01:17:05,537 --> 01:17:09,833
that would be probably clean shaved
with a black bag with an arrow on it.

1270
01:17:09,916 --> 01:17:12,127
We were searching for this guy.

1271
01:17:12,210 --> 01:17:14,296
We didn't know when he was going to come.

1272
01:17:14,379 --> 01:17:16,632
[Smoak] And everybody was
just kind of like,

1273
01:17:17,215 --> 01:17:20,302
"All right, how do you want me
to pick that out of here?"

1274
01:17:20,385 --> 01:17:23,347
You know, there's thousands of people
in front of me.

1275
01:17:23,430 --> 01:17:26,141
We were kind of at a standstill
just holding everybody back,

1276
01:17:26,224 --> 01:17:30,103
waiting to get the final word
if we're pulling out or not.

1277
01:17:32,397 --> 01:17:35,525
And somebody screamed, "IED."

1278
01:17:35,609 --> 01:17:36,693
[explosion]

1279
01:17:44,201 --> 01:17:45,369
The bomb went off.

1280
01:17:48,288 --> 01:17:52,834
I saw a big cloud of smoke,
and an arm flying through the air.

1281
01:17:52,918 --> 01:17:54,544
[people clamoring]

1282
01:17:54,628 --> 01:17:57,297
[Sanchez] My ears are ringing.
I can't hear anything.

1283
01:17:57,381 --> 01:18:00,133
And I start seeing a face in front of me.

1284
01:18:01,134 --> 01:18:02,427
Like, it didn't look alive.

1285
01:18:02,928 --> 01:18:06,598
And I was just like,
I couldn't process who that face was.

1286
01:18:07,391 --> 01:18:12,562
[Smoak] I jumped up and started to run
towards the hole in the fence

1287
01:18:12,646 --> 01:18:14,898
that we had cut through the Abbey Gate.

1288
01:18:14,981 --> 01:18:17,901
An officer grabbed me
and tried to stop me.

1289
01:18:18,402 --> 01:18:20,612
I told him to get
his fucking hands off of me,

1290
01:18:20,696 --> 01:18:22,531
that those were
all of my friends in there.

1291
01:18:23,115 --> 01:18:25,492
And I turned around
and started running towards…

1292
01:18:25,575 --> 01:18:28,495
Now I could see
pretty much just a pile of bodies.

1293
01:18:29,538 --> 01:18:32,124
And the first person that I came to,

1294
01:18:32,207 --> 01:18:36,420
um, was one of my best friends,
uh, Corporal Daegan Page.

1295
01:18:37,462 --> 01:18:40,424
I just knew as soon as I saw him
that he was dead.

1296
01:18:42,175 --> 01:18:45,637
Somebody grabbed one side
of his chest rig, and I grabbed the other

1297
01:18:46,430 --> 01:18:48,473
{\an8}and started dragging him back to the hole

1298
01:18:48,557 --> 01:18:50,016
{\an8}that we had in the fence.

1299
01:18:50,559 --> 01:18:53,395
My captain told me that he had Page

1300
01:18:53,478 --> 01:18:55,647
and told me to go back
and get more people.

1301
01:18:56,481 --> 01:18:58,525
So I left him…

1302
01:19:02,612 --> 01:19:04,197
and, uh…

1303
01:19:04,281 --> 01:19:05,157
[sniffles]

1304
01:19:06,032 --> 01:19:07,075
Sorry.

1305
01:19:09,786 --> 01:19:10,662
[sniffles]

1306
01:19:11,580 --> 01:19:12,539
[clears throat]

1307
01:19:18,462 --> 01:19:25,469
[indistinct clamoring
and dialog in Afghan language]

1308
01:19:26,511 --> 01:19:28,263
-[yelling]
-[sharp snapping sounds]

1309
01:19:28,346 --> 01:19:30,766
And I start hearing screaming
and, like, snaps coming.

1310
01:19:30,849 --> 01:19:31,683
[snapping sounds]

1311
01:19:31,767 --> 01:19:35,645
I thought I was seeing stars or something,
but they looked like flashes.

1312
01:19:35,729 --> 01:19:38,273
My mind immediately went to,
"I'm getting fucking shot at."

1313
01:19:39,441 --> 01:19:41,943
I had gotten shot in my left shoulder.

1314
01:19:44,738 --> 01:19:46,281
I was about 10 yards from the blast.

1315
01:19:47,449 --> 01:19:49,826
The canal wall was right here,

1316
01:19:49,910 --> 01:19:52,537
and then he was
just on the other side of it.

1317
01:19:55,081 --> 01:19:56,875
My right lung had collapsed.

1318
01:19:57,751 --> 01:20:01,505
A bearing hit me in the jaw, in my lat,
and then stitched up on my right side,

1319
01:20:01,588 --> 01:20:05,300
and I was shot
down in the bottom of my right leg.

1320
01:20:06,551 --> 01:20:09,471
And I'm walking out, stumbling out.

1321
01:20:09,554 --> 01:20:11,681
It feels like I'm breathing
through a straw.

1322
01:20:13,767 --> 01:20:16,269
{\an8}Daegan Page and Max Soviak,

1323
01:20:17,312 --> 01:20:19,356
{\an8}those two were inseparable.

1324
01:20:20,357 --> 01:20:21,566
They were just good dudes.

1325
01:20:21,650 --> 01:20:24,820
Everywhere Max went, Page went.
Everywhere Page went, Max went.

1326
01:20:25,904 --> 01:20:30,158
[people clamoring]

1327
01:20:32,244 --> 01:20:36,790
Max and Page were behind me
by a little bit, and then we got hit,

1328
01:20:36,873 --> 01:20:40,293
and when I turned around,
I crawled over somebody.

1329
01:20:40,377 --> 01:20:42,754
And to this day, I'm still convinced

1330
01:20:42,838 --> 01:20:45,590
{\an8}that who I crawled over was Page.

1331
01:20:47,425 --> 01:20:49,177
{\an8}But yeah, they didn't make it.

1332
01:20:51,555 --> 01:20:53,265
[people clamoring in Afghan language]

1333
01:20:56,226 --> 01:20:58,270
That's when I tried to grab Kelsee.

1334
01:20:59,980 --> 01:21:04,025
{\an8}Kelsee Lainhart was part
of the female engagement team.

1335
01:21:04,943 --> 01:21:08,071
I grabbed her,
I drug her as far as I could.

1336
01:21:09,239 --> 01:21:11,783
I've kind of come to the realization,
"This is where I die."

1337
01:21:12,284 --> 01:21:13,201
"Fuck it."

1338
01:21:14,911 --> 01:21:16,037
I just rolled over.

1339
01:21:16,538 --> 01:21:18,331
"This is where it's going to happen."

1340
01:21:18,832 --> 01:21:21,001
Got as comfortable as I could
and laid back.

1341
01:21:21,084 --> 01:21:22,961
[people clamoring]

1342
01:21:23,044 --> 01:21:27,132
[Sanchez] And then I looked to my right,
and there's all this screaming going on,

1343
01:21:27,215 --> 01:21:28,383
and there's, uh…

1344
01:21:28,884 --> 01:21:31,428
I see Wyatt coming in on a stretcher.

1345
01:21:32,429 --> 01:21:35,974
[Wilson] They sent me down in
a dimly lit hallway, while this JSOC doc

1346
01:21:36,057 --> 01:21:37,851
finds me pulseless.

1347
01:21:40,729 --> 01:21:44,983
He takes a shot in the dark
and performs a emergency thoracotomy…

1348
01:21:47,277 --> 01:21:48,653
last-ditch effort.

1349
01:21:50,864 --> 01:21:54,492
They cut through your sternum
and then they hand-pump your heart.

1350
01:21:56,620 --> 01:21:58,580
He got me going again and then,

1351
01:21:59,205 --> 01:22:01,750
I'm not sure how many units of blood
your body holds,

1352
01:22:01,833 --> 01:22:05,503
but whatever they gave me is
the equivalent of an entire body's worth.

1353
01:22:19,142 --> 01:22:23,730
[Smoak] Wilson, he's got a tattoo
on his, I think his shoulder or his arm,

1354
01:22:23,813 --> 01:22:25,732
and it sums up his entire life.

1355
01:22:25,815 --> 01:22:28,109
It says, "I'm too stubborn to die."

1356
01:22:28,193 --> 01:22:31,571
And it's the truth,
because he is a stubborn son of a bitch.

1357
01:22:34,074 --> 01:22:36,284
[Wilson] I did not know he was ISIS,

1358
01:22:36,368 --> 01:22:38,536
but through the investigation
and everything,

1359
01:22:38,620 --> 01:22:40,372
like, when I got back to California,

1360
01:22:40,455 --> 01:22:42,582
I kind of found out it was ISIS.

1361
01:22:42,666 --> 01:22:45,001
And the guy had actually been in jail,

1362
01:22:45,627 --> 01:22:48,922
but he was freed
when the Taliban came through.

1363
01:22:51,549 --> 01:22:52,592
Yeah.

1364
01:22:53,969 --> 01:22:57,430
[Sanchez] We had, uh,
13 service members KIA.

1365
01:23:00,558 --> 01:23:04,646
Seeing Wyatt is like a gift. [laughs]

1366
01:23:04,729 --> 01:23:06,356
Uh, it really is.

1367
01:23:06,439 --> 01:23:09,275
Like, when I saw him on that stretcher…

1368
01:23:12,862 --> 01:23:13,863
[sniffles]

1369
01:23:16,866 --> 01:23:18,660
…and, like, when I see him now,

1370
01:23:19,411 --> 01:23:21,579
like, I don't know,
I just get filled with joy. [laughs]

1371
01:23:21,663 --> 01:23:24,082
Like, I don't know how to explain that.
Like… [sniffles]

1372
01:23:24,165 --> 01:23:26,543
Like, I'm just
so happy he's here. [laughs]

1373
01:23:30,588 --> 01:23:34,050
[Wilson] Two or three days after
I woke up, they wheeled me over there,

1374
01:23:34,134 --> 01:23:35,844
and I got to meet Kelsee.

1375
01:23:37,095 --> 01:23:38,680
Your brain's scrambled,

1376
01:23:38,763 --> 01:23:42,475
so you don't really realize
the significance of everything.

1377
01:23:44,269 --> 01:23:47,022
Thankfully, those guys
actually pulled her out.

1378
01:23:48,064 --> 01:23:49,607
She came to my retirement.

1379
01:23:51,317 --> 01:23:52,610
She was paralyzed.

1380
01:24:01,077 --> 01:24:02,954
[Sanchez] Like, every night
or every other night,

1381
01:24:03,038 --> 01:24:04,581
we would get, like, an intel brief

1382
01:24:04,664 --> 01:24:08,126
of how many people we saved,
like, throughout that week.

1383
01:24:13,089 --> 01:24:14,299
[indistinct chatter over radio]

1384
01:24:14,382 --> 01:24:17,260
Towards the end, it was
like 120,000 people. Like, damn.

1385
01:24:24,267 --> 01:24:26,853
[Smoak] It feels good

1386
01:24:27,812 --> 01:24:29,522
to have done something,

1387
01:24:29,606 --> 01:24:33,735
to have been a part of it,
and to help people's lives.

1388
01:24:33,818 --> 01:24:34,986
It feels good.

1389
01:24:36,029 --> 01:24:38,364
But at the same time,

1390
01:24:39,783 --> 01:24:41,242
what was it worth?

1391
01:24:43,453 --> 01:24:46,956
This might not be the politically
correct answer, but in my opinion,

1392
01:24:48,917 --> 01:24:53,546
I would not have traded a single
Afghan life for any of my friends.

1393
01:24:55,048 --> 01:24:57,592
[somber music playing]

1394
01:25:03,765 --> 01:25:07,560
I noticed it pretty quick that I was like,
I should not have come home.

1395
01:25:08,269 --> 01:25:10,563
I should've been in the hospital
for two more months.

1396
01:25:13,274 --> 01:25:17,737
Thankfully, they forced me in
to go see a psychiatrist,

1397
01:25:17,821 --> 01:25:20,240
because I never
would have done it by myself.

1398
01:25:20,740 --> 01:25:23,368
And still to this day,
I see that same psychiatrist.

1399
01:25:25,036 --> 01:25:28,540
I didn't want to talk about it.
I refused to talk about it. I hated it.

1400
01:25:32,293 --> 01:25:35,421
Then once I realized, like, talking
about it, it's okay to talk about it,

1401
01:25:35,505 --> 01:25:39,092
It's okay to be
vulnerable for five seconds…

1402
01:25:39,175 --> 01:25:41,761
And trust me, I have a huge fucking ego.

1403
01:25:43,012 --> 01:25:45,640
And when I finally do drop it

1404
01:25:46,224 --> 01:25:48,143
and realize it's okay,

1405
01:25:48,810 --> 01:25:51,437
things start getting better really fast.

1406
01:25:58,111 --> 01:25:59,779
-[child] Dada?
-[Wilson] What's up?

1407
01:25:59,863 --> 01:26:01,656
[child] How do you get on this rock?

1408
01:26:01,739 --> 01:26:03,992
-Huh?
-I want to get on that big rock.

1409
01:26:04,075 --> 01:26:06,119
-You're scared on the big rock?
-Hey!

1410
01:26:07,537 --> 01:26:09,831
-Let me down.
-Let you down?

1411
01:26:09,914 --> 01:26:11,249
Dad, stop!

1412
01:26:11,332 --> 01:26:13,334
Stop holding my butt!

1413
01:26:15,295 --> 01:26:17,130
[Wilson] So I had Pops
on my first deployment.

1414
01:26:17,213 --> 01:26:19,716
[indistinct toddler chatter in background]

1415
01:26:20,466 --> 01:26:23,595
And then Pagie… Pagie was born in 2022.

1416
01:26:23,678 --> 01:26:25,221
She's about to be four.

1417
01:26:26,264 --> 01:26:30,935
And then she's named after
Daegan Page and Max Soviak.

1418
01:26:32,228 --> 01:26:36,482
But I shortened Soviak to Sovi, so
it kind of fits her a little bit better.

1419
01:26:38,693 --> 01:26:40,653
[indistinct family chatter]

1420
01:26:40,737 --> 01:26:43,740
Pops lived it.
Yeah, she saw me in the hospital.

1421
01:26:45,450 --> 01:26:47,493
She was two and a half at that point.

1422
01:26:47,577 --> 01:26:49,162
Yeah, she's my little trauma baby.

1423
01:26:49,245 --> 01:26:52,373
We're going through this thing.
We're going through it together.

1424
01:26:52,457 --> 01:26:55,293
She's raising me
as much as I'm raising her.

1425
01:26:55,376 --> 01:26:58,046
[reporter] <i>Scenes of desperation</i>
<i>and chaos in Afghanistan</i>

1426
01:26:58,129 --> 01:27:00,256
<i>are being seen around the world.</i>

1427
01:27:01,633 --> 01:27:03,801
[Wilson] I don't even know
how to explain it.

1428
01:27:04,594 --> 01:27:07,513
I can barely explain it to you guys
in a way that makes sense,

1429
01:27:07,597 --> 01:27:11,184
let alone to an 8- and 9-year-old
when they get to that age.

1430
01:27:11,267 --> 01:27:12,435
-[Poppy] Oh!
-Huh?

1431
01:27:13,186 --> 01:27:16,022
[Page] Daddy, I have these lemons!

1432
01:27:17,440 --> 01:27:18,399
Look!

1433
01:27:22,654 --> 01:27:26,157
[Sanchez] My kids, they've seen me
shirtless with my bullet scar,

1434
01:27:26,241 --> 01:27:28,076
and they've asked me what it is.

1435
01:27:28,159 --> 01:27:31,204
I don't like to sugarcoat it,
because I got shot.

1436
01:27:31,955 --> 01:27:35,541
There are bad people in this world,
and some of them try to hurt you.

1437
01:27:38,878 --> 01:27:41,673
My wife hates it because they're already
saying they want to be Marines

1438
01:27:41,756 --> 01:27:43,967
and put her through that trauma.

1439
01:27:44,592 --> 01:27:47,804
And now we got to see our kids
go through it? That would probably suck.

1440
01:27:51,766 --> 01:27:56,229
[reporter] <i>Since the Taliban regained</i>
<i>control of Afghanistan in 2021,</i>

1441
01:27:56,312 --> 01:28:01,150
<i>some 200,000 Afghans,</i>
<i>many who supported the US war effort,</i>

1442
01:28:01,234 --> 01:28:03,778
<i>have found refuge in the United States.</i>

1443
01:28:06,155 --> 01:28:08,533
I got on the plane
like it was a charter flight

1444
01:28:09,117 --> 01:28:13,788
from Kabul to Bahrain
and from Bahrain to New Jersey.

1445
01:28:14,998 --> 01:28:19,627
In New Jersey,
I've been in a camp for like a month.

1446
01:28:21,754 --> 01:28:24,507
{\an8}Then I left the camp to come to Arizona.

1447
01:28:26,134 --> 01:28:28,594
Right now, I'm doing Amazon Flex,

1448
01:28:28,678 --> 01:28:32,682
like just an hour and two hours,
three hours a day,

1449
01:28:32,765 --> 01:28:37,228
because I can't work properly because
I have my kids all the time on me.

1450
01:28:37,312 --> 01:28:40,064
I'm waiting on my wife until she joins me.

1451
01:28:42,483 --> 01:28:45,528
She is the wife of a former pilot.

1452
01:28:47,196 --> 01:28:51,617
Since 2021,
she's living under the hijab all the time.

1453
01:28:53,453 --> 01:28:56,039
It's the Taliban rules.

1454
01:28:58,291 --> 01:29:01,544
She do not feel good at all.

1455
01:29:03,546 --> 01:29:06,966
You lose your husband, you lose your son,
you lose your daughter.

1456
01:29:07,759 --> 01:29:09,135
What's going to happen to you?

1457
01:29:17,685 --> 01:29:24,067
[Graff] The irony is the existential
threat to American freedom and liberty

1458
01:29:24,150 --> 01:29:28,071
came not from
these terrorists in Afghanistan,

1459
01:29:28,654 --> 01:29:31,407
and not from the threat of terrorism,

1460
01:29:31,491 --> 01:29:35,036
but from our nation's response
to terrorism.

1461
01:29:36,704 --> 01:29:43,461
Bin Laden really believed that he could
never destroy the United States,

1462
01:29:44,128 --> 01:29:46,964
but by attacking the United States,

1463
01:29:47,048 --> 01:29:51,302
by sort of pricking
the edges of the US empire,

1464
01:29:51,386 --> 01:29:55,223
he could cause
the United States to destroy itself.

1465
01:29:56,724 --> 01:30:02,063
If you start to scratch at
almost any aspect of our daily lives

1466
01:30:02,146 --> 01:30:04,816
in the United States today,

1467
01:30:04,899 --> 01:30:08,820
{\an8}I can show you how 9/11 changed
that part of our country.

1468
01:30:08,903 --> 01:30:12,615
-[CBP agents yelling]
-[protesters yelling]

1469
01:30:13,157 --> 01:30:14,325
[people screaming]

1470
01:30:14,409 --> 01:30:17,453
[Hathaway] We see the harm that this does

1471
01:30:17,537 --> 01:30:22,625
to our own sense of legal constraints
on the president's authority.

1472
01:30:22,708 --> 01:30:25,920
We see these military tactics
coming back home.

1473
01:30:26,003 --> 01:30:27,171
[man screaming] What the hell?

1474
01:30:27,922 --> 01:30:30,967
[Hathaway] We see
some of the treatment of Americans

1475
01:30:31,050 --> 01:30:36,889
that looks awfully familiar for people
who've seen the War on Terror abroad.

1476
01:30:36,973 --> 01:30:39,517
-Bro--
-[woman] Get the fuck off him!

1477
01:30:39,600 --> 01:30:43,020
[people yelling]

1478
01:30:43,104 --> 01:30:45,273
{\an8}And now realize,
"Oh, it's a terrible thing

1479
01:30:45,356 --> 01:30:48,901
{\an8}when a single person
can order a use of military force

1480
01:30:48,985 --> 01:30:52,989
or order a use of force that's lethal,
and there's nothing I can do about it."

1481
01:30:53,823 --> 01:30:55,658
[reporter] <i>The Trump administration</i>
<i>now confirms</i>

1482
01:30:55,741 --> 01:30:58,828
<i>it hit an alleged drug boat</i>
<i>not once, but twice,</i>

1483
01:30:58,911 --> 01:31:02,957
<i>the second strike ordered after the first</i>
<i>failed to kill everyone on board.</i>

1484
01:31:05,793 --> 01:31:08,880
{\an8}[Trump] This military operation,
I won't use the word "war,"

1485
01:31:08,963 --> 01:31:13,259
they don't like the word "war,"
because you're supposed to get approval,

1486
01:31:13,342 --> 01:31:15,720
so I'll use the word "military operation."

1487
01:31:16,679 --> 01:31:18,222
[Pete Hegseth] We will finish this

1488
01:31:18,306 --> 01:31:23,186
on America-first conditions
of President Trump's choosing.

1489
01:31:23,895 --> 01:31:26,439
{\an8}Death and destruction from the sky.

1490
01:31:26,522 --> 01:31:28,733
[explosion]

1491
01:31:30,401 --> 01:31:32,695
[Ackerman] People generally think
the War on Terror is over.

1492
01:31:32,778 --> 01:31:35,656
Their political leadership
has told them of that.

1493
01:31:35,740 --> 01:31:40,203
Well, the authorities, the institutions,
and many of the operations persist.

1494
01:31:40,286 --> 01:31:41,621
They just find new targets.

1495
01:31:45,208 --> 01:31:50,046
We should not fool ourselves into thinking
that we have seen its final form.

1496
01:31:50,129 --> 01:31:52,465
It will come for more and more of us.

1497
01:32:01,766 --> 01:32:05,436
[Goldsmith] We really are
at an inflection point in terms of

1498
01:32:05,520 --> 01:32:08,940
there has to be soon a large turnover

1499
01:32:09,023 --> 01:32:13,152
among the very aged politicians
who've been in power for a long time.

1500
01:32:14,987 --> 01:32:16,864
And it is true, just read the polls,

1501
01:32:16,948 --> 01:32:19,700
the younger generation
thinks differently about these things.

1502
01:32:22,078 --> 01:32:24,914
My students literally weren't born then.

1503
01:32:25,456 --> 01:32:30,002
9/11 to them is like,
uh, World War II to me.

1504
01:32:30,503 --> 01:32:33,506
Or even less so,
it's an even dimmer memory.

1505
01:32:34,173 --> 01:32:38,010
They've kind of inherited
the current situation.

1506
01:32:38,594 --> 01:32:41,055
[young woman] We don't know
what it's like to not have

1507
01:32:41,138 --> 01:32:45,226
police officers in the subways
or on every corner.

1508
01:32:45,309 --> 01:32:48,104
It's a lot more scary for us to think that

1509
01:32:48,187 --> 01:32:50,273
if we're in the wrong place
at the wrong time,

1510
01:32:50,356 --> 01:32:52,316
our lives could be affected immensely

1511
01:32:52,400 --> 01:32:54,652
or even just our lives taken away from us.

1512
01:32:55,319 --> 01:32:58,489
[young man] Events like that happen,
things that are so catastrophic,

1513
01:32:58,573 --> 01:33:02,868
they change the world,
but there's nothing we can do about that.

1514
01:33:02,952 --> 01:33:06,539
So we just kind of live
in the different world.

1515
01:33:15,798 --> 01:33:18,467
What did we get
from the war in Afghanistan?

1516
01:33:20,219 --> 01:33:22,430
[Gopal] This is the longest war
in American history,

1517
01:33:22,513 --> 01:33:25,099
and it's also the longest war
in Afghan history.

1518
01:33:26,517 --> 01:33:28,352
So it's this intergenerational trauma.

1519
01:33:30,229 --> 01:33:32,773
I've spoken to a lot
of American soldiers over the years,

1520
01:33:32,857 --> 01:33:37,445
and it's unclear if you talk to somebody
who landed in 2021,

1521
01:33:37,528 --> 01:33:41,907
what they're doing at that moment
has to do with the 9/11 attacks.

1522
01:33:46,162 --> 01:33:48,331
[Wilson] I mean,
we got the guy in charge of it.

1523
01:33:48,414 --> 01:33:50,583
You can say that's a plus,

1524
01:33:51,292 --> 01:33:54,378
but it wasn't until
10 years fucking later.

1525
01:33:55,087 --> 01:33:59,425
[man] At 25 years since 9/11, how do you
make sense of the global War on Terror?

1526
01:34:01,135 --> 01:34:04,388
That's the million-dollar question, right?
Was it worth it?

1527
01:34:11,228 --> 01:34:14,899
[Homer] I feel like
I went through so much,

1528
01:34:14,982 --> 01:34:18,027
and my family went through so much,
when I was so young.

1529
01:34:20,237 --> 01:34:25,826
The fact that I've made it out of it,
like, shocks me a lot.

1530
01:34:29,497 --> 01:34:31,999
I wish I knew what I would have been like

1531
01:34:32,083 --> 01:34:35,211
or what my life
would have been like before,

1532
01:34:35,294 --> 01:34:37,088
but I didn't really get a "before."

1533
01:34:38,422 --> 01:34:43,177
I'm kind of just trying to catch up
with, like, a life that I lost.

1534
01:34:46,555 --> 01:34:49,433
I'm just going to keep
trying to do my best. [laughs]

1535
01:35:00,319 --> 01:35:03,989
Since the 25 years,
our American society has become divisive

1536
01:35:04,073 --> 01:35:06,617
and just not willing
to listen to each other.

1537
01:35:09,370 --> 01:35:12,415
So it is critically important
to have dialogue

1538
01:35:12,498 --> 01:35:15,543
and a constructive way
of resolving differences.

1539
01:35:17,753 --> 01:35:20,506
History will judge
based on what we do right now.

1540
01:35:30,725 --> 01:35:35,479
[solemn music playing]

1541
01:37:46,193 --> 01:37:47,695
[music ends]